window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-chem-organic-basics","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles · Practice","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","subject":"Chemistry","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":1,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"0.32 g of an organic compound was heated with fuming nitric acid in a Carius tube. The sulphuric acid formed gave 0.466 g of barium sulphate. What is the percentage of sulphur in the compound? (Atomic masses: Ba = 137, S = 32, O = 16)","image":"","options":["20.0%","10.0%","40.0%","14.6%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 0.32 g, m (BaSO$_4$) = 0.466 g, molar mass of BaSO$_4$ = 233 g/mol.\nFormula: % S = $\\frac{32 \\times m_1 \\times 100}{233 \\times m}$\nSubstituting: % S = $\\frac{32 \\times 0.466 \\times 100}{233 \\times 0.32}$\nMass of S = 0.064 g, so % S = 20.0%.\nThe option 14.6% comes from dividing 0.466 g of BaSO$_4$ by 0.32 g of sample without converting to sulphur.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":2,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs represents functional group isomers?","image":"","options":["Propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol","Butane and 2-methylpropane","Propanal and propanone","Ethoxyethane and methoxypropane"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Propanal ($CH_3CH_2CHO$) and propanone ($CH_3COCH_3$) share the formula $C_3H_6O$.\nPropanal contains an aldehyde group, while propanone contains a ketone group. Different functional groups make them functional group isomers.\nThe other pairs do not fit:\n(1) Position isomers.\n(2) Chain isomers.\n(4) Metamers, because both are ethers.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":3,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Unbranched alkane names carry a prefix showing the number of carbon atoms. For which members are these prefixes taken from trivial names instead of numerical roots?","image":"","options":["$CH_4$ to $C_3H_8$ only","$CH_4$ to $C_4H_{10}$","$CH_4$ to $C_5H_{12}$","$C_2H_6$ to $C_6H_{14}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Straight-chain alkanes end in '-ane' and carry a prefix giving the number of carbons.\nThe exceptions are methane, ethane, propane and butane ($CH_4$ to $C_4H_{10}$), whose prefixes come from trivial names.\nFrom $C_5H_{12}$ (pentane) onwards, numerical prefixes are used (pent-, hex-, ...).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":4,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Pentane, 2-methylbutane (isopentane) and 2,2-dimethylpropane (neopentane) all have the molecular formula $C_5H_{12}$. These three compounds are related to one another as:","image":"","options":["Chain isomers","Position isomers","Functional group isomers","Metamers"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"All three compounds are alkanes with the same formula, $C_5H_{12}$.\nThey contain no functional group. They differ only in the carbon skeleton: one is a straight chain and two are branched.\nCompounds that differ in the arrangement of the carbon chain are chain (skeletal) isomers.\nPosition isomerism needs a substituent or functional group whose position changes, and none is present here.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":5,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following groups show a negative resonance (–R) effect when attached to a conjugated system?\n$-COOH$, $-OCOR$, $-CHO$, $-NHCOR$, $-CN$, $-OR$, $-NO_2$, $>C=O$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A –R group withdraws π electrons from the conjugated system towards itself.\nThe –R groups here are $-COOH$, $-CHO$, $-CN$, $-NO_2$ and $>C=O$. That makes 5.\n$-OCOR$, $-NHCOR$ and $-OR$ have a lone pair on the atom attached to the system. They donate this pair, so they are +R groups.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":6,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"A compound contains three functional groups: an –OH, an –NH$_2$ and a –CHO group. Following the IUPAC priority order, which group is named as the suffix (principal functional group)?","image":"","options":["–OH","–NH$_2$","–CHO","All three are named as suffixes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In the IUPAC priority order, –CHO ranks above –OH, and –OH ranks above –NH$_2$.\nThe highest-priority group sets the suffix. Here that is –CHO, so the compound is named as an '-al'.\nThe other two groups become prefixes: hydroxy and amino.\n–OH is the tempting wrong choice. It outranks –NH$_2$ but not –CHO.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":7,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In a carbocation such as $CH_3^+$, the positively charged carbon is best described as:","image":"","options":["$sp^3$ hybridised, pyramidal, with a lone pair in one hybrid orbital","$sp^2$ hybridised, trigonal planar, with an unhybridised p orbital left vacant","$sp$ hybridised, linear, with two vacant p orbitals","$sp^2$ hybridised, trigonal planar, with one electron in the unhybridised p orbital"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The cationic carbon forms three σ bonds using three $sp^2$ hybrid orbitals, so its geometry is trigonal planar.\nIts unhybridised p orbital lies perpendicular to the molecular plane and holds no electrons.\nOption (1) describes a carbanion. Option (4) has an electron in the p orbital, which would describe a radical, not a cation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":8,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In a Kjeldahl estimation, 0.40 g of an organic compound was digested. The ammonia evolved was absorbed in 50 mL of 0.5 M $H_2SO_4$. The residual acid required 70 mL of 0.5 M NaOH for neutralisation. The percentage of nitrogen in the compound is:","image":"","options":["52.5%","26.25%","35.0%","70.0%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 0.40 g, V = 50 mL of acid, M = 0.5 M, V₁ = 70 mL of NaOH of molarity M.\nFormula: %N = $\\frac{1.4 \\times M \\times 2(V - V_1/2)}{m}$\nSubstituting: %N = $\\frac{1.4 \\times 0.5 \\times 2(50 - 35)}{0.40} = \\frac{21}{0.40}$\nSo %N = 52.5%.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":9,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (compound) with List-II (class).\nList-I:\n(A) Tetrahydrofuran\n(B) Thiophene\n(C) Cyclohexane\n(D) Tropolone\nList-II:\n(i) Alicyclic heterocyclic compound\n(ii) Aromatic heterocyclic compound\n(iii) Alicyclic carbocyclic (homocyclic) compound\n(iv) Non-benzenoid aromatic compound\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-i, C-iii, D-iv","A-i, B-ii, C-iv, D-iii","A-i, B-ii, C-iii, D-iv","A-iii, B-ii, C-i, D-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(A) Tetrahydrofuran has an O atom in a saturated ring. It is not aromatic, so it is alicyclic heterocyclic (i).\n(B) Thiophene has an S atom in an aromatic five-membered ring, so it is aromatic heterocyclic (ii).\n(C) Cyclohexane has a ring made only of carbon atoms and is not aromatic, so it is alicyclic carbocyclic (iii).\n(D) Tropolone is aromatic but has no benzene ring, so it is non-benzenoid aromatic (iv).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":10,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"A long alkane chain carries the complex substituent $-CH(CH_3)-CH_2-CH(CH_3)_2$. How is this substituent written in the IUPAC name?","image":"","options":["(2,4-Dimethylbutyl)","(1,3-Dimethylbutyl)","(1,3,3-Trimethylpropyl)","(3-Methylbutyl)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A branched substituent is named as a substituted alkyl group. Its numbering starts at the carbon attached to the parent chain, which is C-1.\nTake the longest chain within the branch starting from that carbon: CH–CH2–CH–CH3, which has 4 carbons, so the base name is butyl.\nThe methyl groups are on C-1 and C-3, giving (1,3-dimethylbutyl).\nOption (3) uses a propyl base, which is not the longest chain in the branch. Option (4) leaves out one carbon.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":11,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When testing for halogens in a compound that also contains nitrogen or sulphur, the sodium fusion extract is boiled with concentrated nitric acid before silver nitrate is added.\nReason (R): Boiling with nitric acid decomposes the cyanide and sulphide present in the extract, which would otherwise react with silver nitrate and interfere with the test.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT specifies boiling the extract with conc. $HNO_3$ when N or S is present.\nR is true: $NaCN$ and $Na_2S$ are destroyed on boiling with the acid.\nIf they remained, they would form AgCN or $Ag_2S$ precipitates and could be mistaken for silver halide.\nR therefore explains why the boiling step in A is needed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":12,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"What is the difference in molecular mass between two successive members of a homologous series? (C = 12 u, H = 1 u)","image":"","options":["12 u","14 u","16 u","28 u"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Successive members of a homologous series differ by one –CH$_2$– unit.\nThe mass of –CH$_2$– is 12 + 2(1) = 14 u.\n12 u counts only the carbon atom.\n28 u would be the difference for two –CH$_2$– units, i.e. alternate members.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":13,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: An sp hybridised carbon atom is more electronegative than an $sp^3$ hybridised carbon atom.\nStatement II: An sp hybrid orbital has 50% s character, so its electrons are held closer to the nucleus than in an $sp^3$ orbital.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement II: Correct. An sp orbital has 50% s character, while an $sp^3$ orbital has 25%. s electrons stay closer to the nucleus.\nStatement I: Correct. Greater s character means the carbon holds bonding electrons more strongly. So the order of electronegativity is sp > $sp^2$ > $sp^3$.\nStatement II also explains Statement I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":14,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Ethoxyethane ($C_2H_5OC_2H_5$) and 1-methoxypropane ($CH_3OC_3H_7$) are metamers.\nStatement II: Pentane and 2-methylbutane are position isomers.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Both ethers are $C_4H_{10}O$ and differ only in the alkyl groups on either side of the oxygen, which is metamerism.\nStatement II is incorrect. Pentane and 2-methylbutane differ in their carbon skeleton (straight versus branched), which makes them chain isomers.\nPosition isomers have the same skeleton but a different position of the substituent or functional group.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":15,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When the compound contains nitrogen or sulphur, the sodium fusion extract is boiled with concentrated nitric acid before silver nitrate is added in the test for halogens.\nReason (R): Boiling with nitric acid decomposes sodium cyanide and sodium sulphide. These would otherwise interfere with the silver nitrate test.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: in Lassaigne's test for halogens, the extract is first acidified and boiled with $HNO_3$ when N or S is present.\nR is true: $NaCN$ and $Na_2S$ formed during fusion would also react with $Ag^+$ and give misleading precipitates. Boiling removes them.\nR therefore states exactly why the step in A is carried out.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":16,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In a Carius estimation, 0.30 g of an organic compound gave 0.466 g of barium sulphate. What is the percentage of sulphur in the compound? (Molar mass of $BaSO_4$ = 233 g/mol, S = 32)","image":"","options":["21.33%","10.67%","42.67%","15.53%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 0.30 g, mass of $BaSO_4$ = 0.466 g.\nFormula: % S = $\\frac{32 \\times m_1 \\times 100}{233 \\times m}$.\nSubstituting: % S = $\\frac{32 \\times 0.466 \\times 100}{233 \\times 0.30}$ = 1491.2/69.9.\nSo % S = 21.33%.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":17,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): CH$_3$OH, CH$_3$CH$_2$OH and CH$_3$CH$_2$CH$_2$OH are members of the same homologous series.\nReason (R): Members of a homologous series contain the same functional group, and successive members differ by a –CH$_2$– unit.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: all three compounds are alkanols, each carrying the –OH group.\nEach member differs from the next by one –CH$_2$– unit.\nR is true: it correctly states the definition of a homologous series.\nR explains A, because the shared –OH group and the –CH$_2$– difference are exactly why these three compounds form a series.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":18,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"According to the NCERT order of decreasing priority of functional groups, how many of the following pairs list the higher-priority group FIRST?\n(i) $-COOR$, $-COCl$\n(ii) $-CN$, $-CONH_2$\n(iii) $-CHO$, $>C=O$\n(iv) $-NH_2$, $-OH$\n(v) $-SO_3H$, $-COOR$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The NCERT order is: $-COOH > -SO_3H > -COOR > -COCl > -CONH_2 > -CN > -CHO > >C=O > -OH > -NH_2 > C=C > C\\equiv C$.\n(i) Correct: ester is above acid chloride.\n(ii) Wrong: amide is above nitrile.\n(iii) Correct: aldehyde is above ketone.\n(iv) Wrong: $-OH$ is above $-NH_2$.\n(v) Correct: sulphonic acid is above ester.\nSo 3 pairs are in the correct order.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":19,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In a Kjeldahl estimation, the ammonia evolved from 0.40 g of an organic compound was absorbed in 50 mL of 0.5 M $H_2SO_4$. The residual acid required 70 mL of 0.5 M NaOH for complete neutralisation (N = 14).\nStatement I: The percentage of nitrogen in the compound is 52.5%.\nStatement II: Kjeldahl's method can be applied to all nitrogenous compounds, including nitro compounds, azo compounds and pyridine.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: $H_2SO_4$ provides 50 × 0.5 × 2 = 50 mmol $H^+$. NaOH used = 70 × 0.5 = 35 mmol.\nSo $H^+$ consumed by $NH_3$ = 15 mmol, which gives 15 mmol N = 0.21 g.\n%N = 0.21/0.40 × 100 = 52.5%. Correct.\nStatement II: Nitrogen in nitro groups, azo groups and the pyridine ring is not converted to ammonium sulphate. Incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":20,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Hydrazine ($NH_2NH_2$) contains nitrogen, yet it does not give the Prussian blue colour in Lassaigne's test. The best reason is:","image":"","options":["Hydrazine is too volatile to be fused with sodium","Hydrazine has no carbon, so sodium cyanide cannot form on fusion","The N–N bond prevents nitrogen from reacting with sodium","Hydrazine forms sodium sulphide instead of sodium cyanide"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In Lassaigne's test, nitrogen is detected as NaCN, which forms when carbon and nitrogen of the compound combine with sodium during fusion.\nThe cyanide ion then gives Prussian blue with $Fe^{2+}$/$Fe^{3+}$.\nHydrazine contains no carbon, so no $CN^-$ forms and the test fails.\nOption (3) is tempting, but the N–N bond is not the cause. The absence of carbon is.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":21,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Considering both structural isomers and stereoisomers (including optical isomers), how many isomers in total are possible for $C_4H_{10}O$?","image":"","options":["6","7","8","9"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Alcohols (4):\n- Butan-1-ol\n- Butan-2-ol\n- 2-methylpropan-1-ol\n- 2-methylpropan-2-ol\nEthers (3): methoxypropane, 2-methoxypropane and ethoxyethane. Ethoxyethane and methoxypropane are metamers.\nThis gives 7 structural isomers.\nButan-2-ol has a chiral C-2 carrying H, OH, $CH_3$ and $C_2H_5$, so it exists as two optical isomers. This adds 1.\nTotal = 7 + 1 = 8.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":22,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (purification technique) with List-II (typical application).\nList-I:\n(a) Sublimation\n(b) Steam distillation\n(c) Distillation under reduced pressure\n(d) Fractional distillation\nList-II:\n(i) Recovery of glycerol from spent-lye in the soap industry\n(ii) Separation of aniline from an aniline–water mixture\n(iii) Separating the fractions of crude oil\n(iv) Purifying a solid that passes directly into the vapour state on heating, such as camphor","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)-(iv): Sublimation is used for solids that vaporise without melting.\n(b)-(ii): Steam distillation suits steam-volatile liquids that are immiscible with water, such as aniline.\n(c)-(i): Glycerol decomposes at its boiling point. It is therefore distilled under reduced pressure from spent-lye.\n(d)-(iii): Crude oil fractions have close boiling points and are separated in a fractionating column.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":23,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Both N–O bonds in nitromethane ($CH_3NO_2$) have the same length, which lies between that of an N–O single bond and an N=O double bond.\nReason (R): The actual structure of nitromethane is a resonance hybrid of two canonical structures.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: experimentally, the two N–O bonds in nitromethane are equal in length. Their length is intermediate between a single and a double bond.\nR is true: nitromethane is represented by two canonical forms in which the N=O and N–O⁻ bonds swap positions. The real molecule is their hybrid.\nIn the hybrid each N–O bond has partial double-bond character, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":24,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following species acts as an electrophile?","image":"","options":["$H_2O$","$CH_3C^{+}=O$ (acylium ion)","$CN^-$","$NH_3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An electrophile is electron-seeking and has an electron-deficient centre.\nIn the acylium ion, the carbon is positively charged and seeks electrons, so it is an electrophile.\n$H_2O$ and $NH_3$ have lone pairs and act as nucleophiles.\n$CN^-$ is an electron-rich anion and is also a nucleophile.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":25,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (test performed) with List-II (positive observation).\nList-I:\n(A) Sodium fusion extract acidified with acetic acid + lead acetate\n(B) Sodium fusion extract + sodium nitroprusside\n(C) Extract of a compound containing both N and S + iron(III) chloride\n(D) Compound oxidised with sodium peroxide, boiled with $HNO_3$ + ammonium molybdate\nList-II:\n(i) Yellow colouration/precipitate\n(ii) Blood-red colour\n(iii) Violet colour\n(iv) Black precipitate","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A)–(iv): $S^{2-}$ reacts with lead acetate to give a black precipitate of PbS.\n(B)–(iii): $S^{2-}$ forms violet $[Fe(CN)_5NOS]^{4-}$ with nitroprusside.\n(C)–(ii): $SCN^-$ reacts with $Fe^{3+}$ to give blood-red $[Fe(SCN)]^{2+}$.\n(D)–(i): Phosphate gives yellow ammonium phosphomolybdate, $(NH_4)_3PO_4\\cdot12MoO_3$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":26,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Methane is drawn in wedge-dash form with two C–H bonds as normal lines in the plane of the paper, one C–H bond as a solid wedge and one as a dashed wedge. Which statement about this drawing is correct?","image":"","options":["The wedge and dash bonds both point towards the observer","The H–C–H angle between the wedge bond and the dash bond is 180°","The plane containing the wedge and dash bonds is perpendicular to the plane of the paper","All four hydrogen atoms actually lie in the plane of the paper"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The carbon in methane is $sp^3$ hybridised and tetrahedral, with H–C–H angles of about 109.5°. This rules out option (2).\nThe two normal lines define one H–C–H plane, which is the paper.\nThe wedge (towards the viewer) and the dash (away from the viewer) form a second H–C–H plane.\nIn a tetrahedron, these two planes are mutually perpendicular. So option (3) is correct.\nOption (1) fails because a dash points away from the observer. Option (4) fails because the wedge and dash bonds leave the plane.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":27,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"A mixture of three compounds P, Q and R is loaded at the top of an alumina column and eluted with a suitable solvent. Their adsorption on alumina follows the order P > Q > R. Which statement is correct?","image":"","options":["P comes out of the column first because it interacts most with the stationary phase","R moves down fastest and is eluted first, while P stays nearest the top of the column","All three compounds come out together because they share the same mobile phase","Q is eluted first because it has intermediate adsorption"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Column chromatography is a form of adsorption chromatography: compounds are adsorbed to different extents on the stationary phase (alumina).\nThe most readily adsorbed compound (P) is held near the top of the column.\nThe least adsorbed compound (R) is carried down fastest by the eluant and comes out first.\nOption (1) reverses this logic: strong adsorption slows a compound down, it does not speed it up.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":28,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Kjeldahl's method can be used to estimate nitrogen in which of the following compounds?","image":"","options":["Nitrobenzene","Pyridine","Azobenzene","Acetamide"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Kjeldahl's method requires the nitrogen to be converted into ammonium sulphate on heating with conc. $H_2SO_4$.\nNitrogen in nitro groups, azo groups and in a ring (as in pyridine) is not converted to ammonium sulphate under these conditions.\nThe amide nitrogen of acetamide is readily converted to ammonium sulphate.\nSo acetamide is the only compound here suited to the method.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":29,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In a reaction mechanism, a curved arrow with only a single barb on its head (a half-headed or 'fish-hook' arrow) is drawn from a covalent bond towards one of the bonded atoms. What does this arrow indicate?","image":"","options":["Movement of an electron pair, as in heterolytic cleavage","Movement of a single electron, as in homolytic cleavage","Transfer of a proton from one atom to another","Migration of an alkyl group during rearrangement"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A full-headed curved arrow shows the movement of an electron pair. This is how heterolytic cleavage is drawn.\nA half-headed (fish-hook) arrow shows the movement of one electron.\nIn homolytic cleavage, each bonded atom takes one electron of the shared pair, which gives free radicals. Two fish-hook arrows are drawn for this.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":30,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Propan-1-ol ($CH_3CH_2CH_2OH$) and methoxyethane ($CH_3OCH_2CH_3$) both have the molecular formula $C_3H_8O$. What type of isomerism do they show?","image":"","options":["Position isomerism","Functional group isomerism","Chain isomerism","Metamerism"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Both compounds have the formula $C_3H_8O$, but they belong to different classes.\nPropan-1-ol has an –OH (alcohol) group. Methoxyethane has a C–O–C (ether) linkage.\nWhen isomers differ in their functional group, they are called functional group isomers.\nPosition isomerism is the tempting wrong choice. It would need the same functional group at different positions, as in propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":31,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In the methyl carbocation, $CH_3^+$, the vacant orbital is an unhybridised p orbital perpendicular to the molecular plane.\n(ii) The methyl carbanion, $CH_3^-$, has an $sp^3$ carbon and a trigonal pyramidal shape.\n(iii) Because of hyperconjugation, the ethyl carbocation is more stable than the isopropyl carbocation.\n(iv) The electromeric effect is a permanent effect that operates even when no attacking reagent is present.\n(v) The –CHO group shows a –R (negative resonance) effect.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $CH_3^+$ is $sp^2$ and planar, and the empty p orbital lies perpendicular to the plane.\n(ii) Correct: $CH_3^-$ is $sp^3$, with the lone pair occupying one corner, giving a pyramidal shape.\n(iii) Incorrect: isopropyl has 6 α-H atoms and ethyl has 3, so isopropyl is more stabilised by hyperconjugation.\n(iv) Incorrect: the electromeric effect is temporary and acts only in the presence of an attacking reagent.\n(v) Correct: –CHO withdraws π electrons, so it shows –R.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":32,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"An organic compound X gives the following results:\n(a) Heated with CuO, it gives a gas that turns lime water milky.\n(b) Its sodium fusion extract, boiled with iron(II) sulphate and acidified with conc. $H_2SO_4$, gives a Prussian blue colour.\n(c) The extract, acidified with acetic acid, gives no black precipitate with lead acetate.\n(d) The extract, boiled with conc. $HNO_3$ and treated with $AgNO_3$, gives a white precipitate soluble in ammonium hydroxide.\nX is most likely:","image":"","options":["Chlorobenzene","4-Chloroaniline","4-Bromoaniline","Sulphanilic acid (4-aminobenzenesulphonic acid)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Carbon is present, which is true of all the options.\n(b) Nitrogen is present, which rules out chlorobenzene.\n(c) Sulphur is absent, which rules out sulphanilic acid.\n(d) A white precipitate soluble in $NH_4OH$ is AgCl, so chlorine is present. 4-Bromoaniline would give pale yellow AgBr, which is only sparingly soluble.\nOnly 4-chloroaniline contains C, H, N and Cl without S.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":33,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many isomers in total are possible for the molecular formula $C_4H_{10}O$, counting all structural isomers (alcohols and ethers) and also stereoisomers?","image":"","options":["7","6","9","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Alcohols (4): butan-1-ol, butan-2-ol, 2-methylpropan-1-ol and 2-methylpropan-2-ol.\nEthers (3): 1-methoxypropane, 2-methoxypropane and ethoxyethane. Ethoxyethane and the methoxypropanes are metamers.\nSo there are 7 structural isomers.\nButan-2-ol has a chiral C-2 carrying H, OH, $CH_3$ and $C_2H_5$. It therefore exists as two optical isomers (enantiomers), which adds one more.\nTotal = 7 + 1 = 8.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":34,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Which pair of compounds are consecutive members of the same homologous series?","image":"","options":["CH$_3$CHO and CH$_3$COCH$_3$","CH$_3$COOH and CH$_3$CH$_2$COOH","CH$_3$OCH$_3$ and CH$_3$CH$_2$OH","HCOOH and CH$_3$COOCH$_3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Consecutive homologues must have the same functional group and differ by exactly one –CH$_2$– unit.\n(1) These differ by CH$_2$, but CH$_3$CHO is an aldehyde and CH$_3$COCH$_3$ is a ketone. They belong to different series.\n(2) Both are alkanoic acids (–COOH) and differ by one CH$_2$. This pair is correct.\n(3) These have the same formula, C$_2$H$_6$O, so they are isomers, not homologues.\n(4) HCOOH is an acid and CH$_3$COOCH$_3$ is an ester, so the functional groups differ.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":35,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (separation required) with List-II (most suitable method).\nList-I:\n(A) Glycerol from spent-lye in the soap industry\n(B) Aniline from an aniline–water mixture\n(C) Different fractions of crude oil in the petroleum industry\n(D) Chloroform (b.p. 334 K) from aniline (b.p. 457 K)\nList-II:\n(I) Steam distillation\n(II) Distillation under reduced pressure\n(III) Simple distillation\n(IV) Fractional distillation","image":"","options":["A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A)-II: Glycerol decomposes at its boiling point. Lowering the pressure lowers its boiling point, so it is distilled under reduced pressure.\n(B)-I: Aniline is steam volatile and immiscible with water, so steam distillation is used.\n(C)-IV: Crude oil fractions have close boiling points and need a fractionating column with many theoretical plates.\n(D)-III: The boiling points differ by about 123 K, so simple distillation is enough.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":36,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Fusion with sodium converts covalently bonded nitrogen, sulphur and halogens of an organic compound into ionic forms.\n(ii) Nitrogen of the compound appears in the sodium fusion extract as sodium cyanide.\n(iii) In the Prussian blue test, some iron(II) ions are oxidised to iron(III) ions on heating.\n(iv) For the lead acetate test of sulphur, the extract is first acidified with nitric acid.\n(v) For phosphorus, the compound is heated with sodium peroxide so that phosphorus is oxidised to phosphate.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: covalent elements become ionic species such as $NaCN$, $Na_2S$ and $NaX$.\n(ii) Correct: $Na + C + N \\rightarrow NaCN$.\n(iii) Correct: the $Fe^{3+}$ formed is needed to produce $Fe_4[Fe(CN)_6]_3$.\n(iv) Incorrect: the extract is acidified with acetic acid, not nitric acid.\n(v) Correct: sodium peroxide oxidises P to phosphate, which is then tested with ammonium molybdate.\nCorrect statements = 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":37,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The C–H bond in ethyne is shorter than the C–H bond in ethane.\n(ii) An sp hybrid orbital has 50% s-character.\n(iii) In a $\\pi$ bond, the electron cloud lies above and below the plane of the bonded atoms.\n(iv) Free rotation about the C=C bond in ethene is possible because the $\\sigma$ bond is cylindrically symmetric.\n(v) An $sp^2$ hybridised carbon is less electronegative than an $sp^3$ hybridised carbon.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the sp orbital in ethyne has more s-character, so it is held closer to the nucleus and forms a shorter bond than the $sp^3$ orbital in ethane.\n(ii) Correct: one s and one p orbital mix, so the s-character is 50%.\n(iii) Correct: the $\\pi$ bond forms by sideways overlap of parallel p orbitals, so its electron cloud lies above and below the molecular plane.\n(iv) Incorrect: rotation would break the p-orbital overlap of the $\\pi$ bond, so rotation is restricted.\n(v) Incorrect: $sp^2$ carbon has 33% s-character versus 25% for $sp^3$, so it is more electronegative.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":38,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In column chromatography, the component that is least strongly adsorbed on the adsorbent is eluted first.\nStatement II: In paper chromatography, the component having greater affinity for the water held in the paper travels farther up the paper and shows a larger $R_f$ value.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. A weakly adsorbed component is held least by the stationary phase, so the eluant carries it down fastest.\nStatement II is incorrect. In paper chromatography, the water trapped in the paper is the stationary phase.\nA component with greater affinity for this water is retained more strongly. So it travels less distance and has a smaller $R_f$.\nComponents that prefer the mobile (solvent) phase move farther.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":39,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"A mixture is spotted on a thin layer chromatography plate and developed in a suitable solvent. The solvent front travels 8.0 cm from the base line. The spot of one component travels 3.6 cm from the base line. What is the retardation factor ($R_f$) of this component?","image":"","options":["0.45","0.55","2.22","0.80"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: distance moved by the substance = 3.6 cm; distance moved by the solvent front = 8.0 cm.\nFormula: $R_f = \\frac{\\text{distance moved by substance from base line}}{\\text{distance moved by solvent from base line}}$\nSubstituting: $R_f = \\frac{3.6}{8.0}$\nSo $R_f$ = 0.45. It has no unit and is always less than 1.\nOption (3) comes from inverting the ratio, which would wrongly give a value greater than 1.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":40,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about chromatography are correct?\n(i) Silica gel and alumina are commonly used as adsorbents in adsorption chromatography.\n(ii) On a TLC plate, the component that is more strongly adsorbed travels farther from the base line.\n(iii) Paper chromatography is partition chromatography in which water trapped in the paper acts as the stationary phase.\n(iv) Colourless amino acid spots on a TLC plate can be made visible by spraying ninhydrin solution.\n(v) A component that is very soluble in the mobile phase can have an $R_f$ value greater than 1.\n(vi) In column chromatography, the most readily adsorbed component is retained near the top of the column.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: silica gel and alumina are standard adsorbents.\n(ii) Incorrect: the less strongly adsorbed component moves farther and has the higher $R_f$.\n(iii) Correct: special chromatography paper holds water, which acts as the stationary phase.\n(iv) Correct: ninhydrin develops coloured spots with amino acids.\n(v) Incorrect: a solute cannot move beyond the solvent front, so $R_f \\le 1$.\n(vi) Correct: strongly adsorbed matter stays at the top, while weakly adsorbed matter elutes first.\nCorrect statements: 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":41,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (mixture to be separated) with List-II (most suitable technique).\nList-I:\n(A) Chloroform (b.p. 334 K) and aniline (b.p. 457 K)\n(B) Fractions of crude oil\n(C) Aniline from an aniline–water mixture\n(D) An organic compound dissolved in water, using an immiscible organic solvent\nList-II:\n(I) Steam distillation\n(II) Differential extraction\n(III) Simple distillation\n(IV) Fractional distillation","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The boiling points differ widely (334 K vs 457 K), so simple distillation works → III.\n(B) Crude oil components have close boiling points, so a fractionating column is needed → IV.\n(C) Aniline is steam volatile and immiscible with water → steam distillation, I.\n(D) Shaking the solution with an immiscible organic solvent in a separatory funnel is differential extraction → II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":42,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"An organic compound contains only carbon, hydrogen and oxygen. On complete combustion, 0.30 g of it gave 0.44 g of $CO_2$ and 0.18 g of $H_2O$. What is the percentage of oxygen in the compound (determined by difference)? (Atomic masses: C = 12, H = 1, O = 16)","image":"","options":["53.33%","40.00%","46.67%","60.00%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 0.30 g, mass of $CO_2$ = 0.44 g, mass of $H_2O$ = 0.18 g.\n% C = $\\frac{12 \\times 0.44 \\times 100}{44 \\times 0.30}$ = 0.12 × 100/0.30 = 40.00%\n% H = $\\frac{2 \\times 0.18 \\times 100}{18 \\times 0.30}$ = 0.02 × 100/0.30 = 6.67%\nThe compound has only C, H and O, so % O = 100 − (40.00 + 6.67) = 53.33%.\nOption (2) is the % of carbon, not oxygen.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":43,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"0.45 g of an organic compound containing only C, H and O gave 0.66 g of CO$_2$ and 0.27 g of H$_2$O on complete combustion. What is the percentage of oxygen in the compound, found by difference? (Atomic masses: C = 12, H = 1, O = 16)","image":"","options":["53.3%","46.7%","40.0%","60.0%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Mass of C = $\\frac{12 \\times 0.66}{44}$ = 0.18 g, so % C = 40.0%.\nMass of H = $\\frac{2 \\times 0.27}{18}$ = 0.03 g, so % H = 6.67%.\n% O = 100 − (40.0 + 6.67) = 53.3%\nThis matches the empirical formula CH$_2$O.\n46.7% is the sum of % C and % H, not % O.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":44,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (separation) with List-II (technique used):\nList-I\n(a) Glycerol from spent-lye in the soap industry\n(b) Aniline from an aniline–water mixture\n(c) Different fractions of crude oil\n(d) Chloroform (b.p. 334 K) from aniline (b.p. 457 K)\nList-II\n(i) Steam distillation\n(ii) Simple distillation\n(iii) Distillation under reduced pressure\n(iv) Fractional distillation","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Glycerol decomposes at its boiling point, so it is distilled under reduced pressure: (iii).\n(b) Aniline is steam volatile and immiscible with water, so steam distillation is used: (i).\n(c) Crude oil fractions have close boiling points, so fractional distillation is used: (iv).\n(d) Chloroform and aniline have widely different boiling points, so simple distillation works: (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":45,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In 1828, F. Wöhler made an organic compound from an inorganic substance. This weakened the vital force theory. Which compound did he make, and from what?","image":"","options":["Acetic acid from carbon","Urea from ammonium cyanate","Methane from carbon and hydrogen","Urea from ammonium carbonate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Wöhler heated ammonium cyanate, $NH_4CNO$, and obtained urea, $NH_2CONH_2$.\nAmmonium cyanate is an inorganic salt, so this showed an organic compound could be made outside a living body.\nKolbe (1845) synthesised acetic acid and Berthelot (1856) synthesised methane, so options 1 and 3 belong to other chemists.\nAmmonium carbonate is not the starting material Wöhler used.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":46,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following species are electrophiles?\n$NO_2^+$, $H_2O$, $(CH_3)_3C^+$, $CN^-$, $BF_3$, $NH_3$, $CH_3\\overset{+}{C}{=}O$, $C_2H_5O^-$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Electrophiles are electron seekers: cations or neutral species with an electron-deficient centre.\nElectrophiles: $NO_2^+$, $(CH_3)_3C^+$, $BF_3$ (incomplete octet on boron) and the acylium ion $CH_3CO^+$. That makes 4.\nNucleophiles: $H_2O$ and $NH_3$ (lone pairs), $CN^-$ and $C_2H_5O^-$ (anions).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":47,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (observation) with List-II (electronic effect mainly responsible).\nList-I\n(A) Both N–O bonds of nitromethane have the same length\n(B) $Cl_2CHCOOH$ is a stronger acid than $ClCH_2COOH$\n(C) $(CH_3)_3C^+$ is more stable than $CH_3^+$\n(D) The π electrons of ethene shift completely towards the carbon attacked by $H^+$\nList-II\n(I) Hyperconjugation\n(II) +E effect\n(III) Resonance\n(IV) –I effect","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The equal N–O bond lengths show that nitromethane is a resonance hybrid, so A–III.\n(B) Two Cl atoms withdraw more electron density than one by the –I effect. This stabilises the carboxylate ion more, so B–IV.\n(C) The tert-butyl cation has nine α-H atoms for hyperconjugation, while $CH_3^+$ has none, so C–I.\n(D) The π electrons are transferred to the atom that the reagent attacks. This is the +E effect, so D–II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":48,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In Liebig's combustion method, 0.30 g of an organic compound gave 0.44 g of carbon dioxide. What is the percentage of carbon in the compound? (Atomic masses: C = 12, O = 16)","image":"","options":["40.0%","20.0%","13.3%","44.0%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m (compound) = 0.30 g, m (CO$_2$) = 0.44 g, molar mass of CO$_2$ = 44 g/mol.\nFormula: % C = $\\frac{12 \\times m_{CO_2} \\times 100}{44 \\times m}$\nSubstituting: % C = $\\frac{12 \\times 0.44 \\times 100}{44 \\times 0.30}$ = $\\frac{528}{13.2}$\nSo % C = 40.0%.\nThe option 44.0% comes from forgetting the 12/44 factor.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":49,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Hyperconjugation, also called 'no-bond resonance', involves the delocalisation of:","image":"","options":["π-electrons of a C=C bond into an adjacent σ* orbital","σ-electrons of a C–H bond of an alkyl group into an adjacent empty p orbital or π system","Lone-pair electrons of a heteroatom into a conjugated π system","Unpaired electrons of a free radical into a lone pair orbital"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Hyperconjugation is the delocalisation of σ-electrons of a C–H bond on the alkyl carbon directly attached to an unsaturated system.\nThe acceptor can also be an atom with an unshared (empty) p orbital, as in a carbocation.\nIn the contributing structures there is no formal bond between that carbon and the H atom, hence the name 'no-bond resonance'.\nDelocalisation of a heteroatom's lone pair into a π system is the resonance effect, not hyperconjugation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":50,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In Dumas method, 0.25 g of an organic compound gave 40 mL of nitrogen collected over water at 300 K and 725 mm Hg pressure. The aqueous tension at 300 K is 25 mm Hg. What is the percentage of nitrogen in the compound? (Volume of 28 g $N_2$ at STP = 22400 mL; STP = 273 K, 760 mm Hg)","image":"","options":["16.76%","17.36%","8.38%","18.42%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: P(dry $N_2$) = 725 − 25 = 700 mm, V = 40 mL, T = 300 K.\nFormula: $V_{STP} = \\frac{273 \\times P \\times V}{T \\times 760}$, and % N = $\\frac{28 \\times V_{STP} \\times 100}{22400 \\times m}$.\nSubstituting: $V_{STP}$ = (273 × 700 × 40)/(300 × 760) = 33.53 mL.\nMass of $N_2$ = 28 × 33.53/22400 = 0.0419 g, so % N = 0.0419/0.25 × 100 = 16.76%.\nIgnoring aqueous tension gives 17.36%, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":51,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are correct IUPAC names?\n2-Ethylpentane, 3-Methylpentane, 1-Methylbutane, 4-Methylhexane, 2,2-Dimethylbutane, 3,3-Dimethylpentane","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"2-Ethylpentane: incorrect. The chain through the ethyl group has 6 carbons, so the name is 3-methylhexane.\n3-Methylpentane: correct.\n1-Methylbutane: incorrect. It is simply pentane.\n4-Methylhexane: incorrect. Lowest locants give 3-methylhexane.\n2,2-Dimethylbutane: correct.\n3,3-Dimethylpentane: correct (the locant is 3 from either end).\nSo 3 names are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":52,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Hyperconjugation, which involves delocalisation of σ electrons of a C–H bond of an alkyl group into an adjacent unsaturated system or empty p orbital, is also known as:","image":"","options":["Electromeric effect","No-bond resonance","Mesomeric effect","Inductive effect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In hyperconjugation, σ electrons of a C–H bond are delocalised into an adjacent π system or empty p orbital.\nIn some contributing structures, there is no formal bond between that carbon and hydrogen. Hence it is called no-bond resonance.\n'Mesomeric effect' is the other name for the resonance effect, which involves π electrons or lone pairs, not σ electrons. So option (3) is the tempting wrong answer.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":53,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"A benzene ring carries three substituents: –Br, –Cl and –NO$_2$. The –Br and –Cl groups are on adjacent carbons, and –NO$_2$ is para to –Br. What is the correct IUPAC name?","image":"","options":["1-bromo-2-chloro-4-nitrobenzene","2-bromo-1-chloro-5-nitrobenzene","4-bromo-3-chloro-1-nitrobenzene","1-chloro-2-bromo-4-nitrobenzene"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"All three groups are prefix-only, so the parent name is benzene.\nTry each starting point and look for the lowest set of locants:\n• Starting at Br and moving toward Cl gives {1,2,4}.\n• Starting at Cl gives {1,2,5}.\n• Starting at NO$_2$ gives {1,3,4}.\nThe lowest set is {1,2,4}, with Br at 1, Cl at 2 and NO$_2$ at 4.\nPrefixes are then listed alphabetically: bromo, chloro, nitro.\nOption (4) has the right locants, but chloro is written before bromo, which breaks alphabetical order.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":54,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"What percentage s-character does an $sp^2$ hybrid orbital of carbon have?","image":"","options":["25%","50%","66.7%","33.3%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"An $sp^2$ orbital is formed by mixing one s and two p orbitals.\nIts s-character is therefore $\\frac{1}{3}$, which is about 33.3%.\nBy the same reasoning, $sp^3$ has 25% s-character and $sp$ has 50%.\n66.7% is the p-character of an $sp^2$ orbital, not its s-character.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":55,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In the three-dimensional wedge-and-dash representation of an organic molecule, what does a solid wedge drawn from a carbon atom indicate?","image":"","options":["A bond lying in the plane of the paper","A bond projecting towards the observer, out of the plane of the paper","A bond projecting away from the observer, behind the plane of the paper","A double bond between the two atoms"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In wedge-and-dash drawings, normal lines are bonds in the plane of the paper.\nA solid wedge is a bond coming out of the plane, towards the observer.\nA dashed wedge is a bond going away from the observer, behind the plane.\nOption (3) describes the dashed wedge, not the solid wedge.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":56,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"A bond-line formula shows a zigzag chain of eight carbon atoms (including both ends). A single short line branches off from the third carbon of the chain. No other atoms or groups are shown. What is the molecular formula of the compound?","image":"","options":["$C_8H_{18}$","$C_9H_{18}$","$C_9H_{20}$","$C_8H_{16}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The main chain has 8 carbons. The branch line ends in a terminal, which is a $CH_3$ group. Total carbons = 9.\nNo double bonds or rings are present, so the compound is a saturated alkane, $C_nH_{2n+2}$.\nFor n = 9 this gives $C_9H_{20}$. The compound is 3-methyloctane.\n$C_8H_{18}$ ignores the branch carbon.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":57,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many $sp^2$ hybridised carbon atoms are present in $HC \\equiv C–CH=CH–CHO$?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Number the carbons C1 to C5 from left to right.\nC1 and C2 form the triple bond, so both are $sp$.\nC3 and C4 form the C=C double bond, so both are $sp^2$.\nC5 is the aldehyde carbon with a C=O double bond, so it is $sp^2$.\nThe total number of $sp^2$ carbons is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":58,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In benzene, all six carbon–carbon bonds have the same length. Given that a C–C single bond is 154 pm and a C=C double bond is 134 pm, the C–C bond length in benzene is about:","image":"","options":["134 pm","139 pm","147 pm","154 pm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Benzene is a resonance hybrid of the two Kekulé structures.\nIn the hybrid, every C–C bond is identical and has partial double-bond character.\nIts length is therefore intermediate between a single and a double bond, about 139 pm.\nThe values 134 pm and 154 pm would apply only to the hypothetical canonical forms, which have alternating double and single bonds.\n147 pm is not the observed value.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":59,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"The Prussian blue colour obtained in Lassaigne's test for nitrogen is due to the formation of:","image":"","options":["$Na_4[Fe(CN)_6]$","$[Fe(SCN)]^{2+}$","$Fe_4[Fe(CN)_6]_3\\cdot xH_2O$","$Na_4[Fe(CN)_5NOS]$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The sodium fusion extract contains $CN^-$. Boiling it with iron(II) sulphate forms hexacyanidoferrate(II): $Fe^{2+} + 6CN^- \\rightarrow [Fe(CN)_6]^{4-}$.\nOn heating, some $Fe^{2+}$ is oxidised to $Fe^{3+}$. The $Fe^{3+}$ then combines: $3[Fe(CN)_6]^{4-} + 4Fe^{3+} \\rightarrow Fe_4[Fe(CN)_6]_3\\cdot xH_2O$, which is Prussian blue.\n$Na_4[Fe(CN)_6]$ is colourless/pale, so it fails.\n$[Fe(SCN)]^{2+}$ is the blood-red species.\nThe nitroprusside complex gives violet colour in the sulphur test.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":60,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Hydrazine ($NH_2NH_2$) contains nitrogen but does not give the Prussian blue colour in Lassaigne's test. What is the best reason?","image":"","options":["It has no carbon, so sodium cyanide cannot form during sodium fusion","Its nitrogen is converted to sodium sulphide, which masks the test","The Prussian blue forms but dissolves completely when the extract is acidified","Its N–N bond produces sodium nitrate instead of a cyanide"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In Lassaigne's test, sodium fusion turns nitrogen into $NaCN$. Both carbon and nitrogen of the compound are needed for this ($Na + C + N \\rightarrow NaCN$).\nThe cyanide ion then gives sodium hexacyanidoferrate(II), which forms Prussian blue with $Fe^{3+}$.\nHydrazine contains no carbon, so no $CN^-$ is formed and no Prussian blue appears.\nOption (2) fails because hydrazine contains no sulphur. Option (3) fails because Prussian blue is formed in acid medium; acid does not dissolve it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":61,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (element being estimated) with List-II (form in which it is finally measured):\nList-I\n(a) Halogen by Carius method\n(b) Sulphur\n(c) Phosphorus\n(d) Nitrogen by Dumas method\nList-II\n(i) $Mg_2P_2O_7$\n(ii) Volume of $N_2$ gas collected over KOH solution\n(iii) AgX\n(iv) $BaSO_4$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Carius method: the halogen is precipitated with $AgNO_3$ and weighed as AgX, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) Sulphur is oxidised to $H_2SO_4$ and weighed as $BaSO_4$, so (b)-(iv).\n(c) Phosphorus is converted to phosphoric acid and weighed as $Mg_2P_2O_7$ (or as ammonium phosphomolybdate), so (c)-(i).\n(d) Dumas method: the $N_2$ produced is collected over KOH solution and its volume is measured, so (d)-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":62,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In a Carius determination, 0.20 g of an organic compound gave 0.282 g of silver bromide. What is the percentage of bromine in the compound? (Atomic masses: Ag = 108, Br = 80)","image":"","options":["60.0 %","30.0 %","42.5 %","75.0 %"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: mass of compound m = 0.20 g; mass of AgBr = 0.282 g; molar mass of AgBr = 108 + 80 = 188 g/mol.\nFormula: % Br = $\\frac{80 \\times m_{AgBr} \\times 100}{188 \\times m}$\nSubstituting: % Br = $\\frac{80 \\times 0.282 \\times 100}{188 \\times 0.20} = \\frac{2256}{37.6}$\nSo the percentage of bromine = 60.0 %.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":63,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In a bond-line structural formula, unless a functional group is indicated, the free ends (terminals) of the lines represent:","image":"","options":["Hydrogen atoms","Methylene ($CH_2$) groups","Methyl ($CH_3$) groups","Methine ($CH$) groups"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In bond-line formulas, carbon and hydrogen atoms are not written.\nThe lines are drawn in a zigzag fashion.\nEach terminal (free end) represents a methyl group, unless a functional group is shown there.\nA junction joining two lines is a $CH_2$ carbon. Hydrogen atoms are never shown as line ends.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":64,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In the common naming system for disubstituted benzenes, the prefix 'para' (p-) corresponds to which pair of IUPAC locants?","image":"","options":["1,2","1,3","1,4","1,5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The common names map to IUPAC locants as follows:\northo (o-) = 1,2\nmeta (m-) = 1,3\npara (p-) = 1,4\nFor example, p-dichlorobenzene is 1,4-dichlorobenzene. A 1,5 pattern is the same as 1,3 (meta), because the ring is numbered to give the lowest locants.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":65,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many carbon atoms are sp hybridised in the molecule $HC\\equiv C-CH=CH-C\\equiv N$?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"C1 and C2 form the $C\\equiv C$ triple bond, so both are sp.\nC3 and C4 form the $C=C$ double bond, so both are $sp^2$.\nC5 is the nitrile carbon in $-C\\equiv N$ and is sp.\nThe sp carbons are therefore C1, C2 and C5, a total of 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":66,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following acids in decreasing order of acid strength:\n(P) $CH_3CH_2CHClCOOH$ (2-chlorobutanoic acid)\n(Q) $CH_3CHClCH_2COOH$ (3-chlorobutanoic acid)\n(R) $ClCH_2CH_2CH_2COOH$ (4-chlorobutanoic acid)\n(S) $CH_3CH_2CH_2COOH$ (butanoic acid)","image":"","options":["P > Q > R > S","R > Q > P > S","S > R > Q > P","P > R > Q > S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Cl is a –I group. It stabilises the carboxylate ion by withdrawing electron density, which increases acid strength.\nThe inductive effect decreases sharply as the distance from –COOH increases.\nCl is closest to –COOH in P, farther in Q, and farthest in R. So the stabilisation order is P > Q > R.\nButanoic acid (S) has no –I substituent, and its alkyl chain is +I. So it is the weakest acid.\nThe order is P > Q > R > S.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":67,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"What is the correct IUPAC name of $CH_3-CH(CH_3)-CH_2-CH(CH_2CH_3)-CH_3$?","image":"","options":["4-Ethyl-2-methylpentane","2-Methyl-4-ethylpentane","2,4-Dimethylhexane","3,5-Dimethylhexane"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: Find the longest chain. If the chain runs through the ethyl group, it contains 6 carbons. The written 5-carbon chain is not the longest.\nStep 2: Number the 6-carbon chain from the end that gives the branches the lowest locants. The two methyl groups then sit at C-2 and C-4, not 3 and 5.\nThe name is 2,4-dimethylhexane.\nOptions (1) and (2) wrongly choose a pentane parent chain.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":68,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following groups is always written as a prefix and never as a suffix in an IUPAC name?","image":"","options":["–CN","–NO$_2$","–OH","–COCl"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Halogens (halo), the nitro group (nitro) and alkoxy groups (alkoxy) are prefix-only. They are never treated as the principal functional group.\n–CN can be a suffix ('-nitrile'), –OH can be a suffix ('-ol') and –COCl can be a suffix ('-oyl chloride').\nSo –NO$_2$ is always written as the prefix 'nitro'.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":69,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: An $sp$ hybridised carbon is more electronegative than an $sp^3$ hybridised carbon.\nStatement II: An orbital with greater s-character holds its electrons closer to the nucleus.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement II is correct. s orbitals lie closer to the nucleus than p orbitals, so more s-character keeps the electrons nearer the nucleus.\nStatement I is correct. An $sp$ orbital (50% s) attracts electrons more strongly than an $sp^3$ orbital (25% s).\nThe order of electronegativity is $sp > sp^2 > sp^3$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":70,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are acyclic (aliphatic) compounds?\nEthane, isobutane, acetaldehyde, acetic acid, cyclopropane, benzene, tetrahydrofuran, acetone","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Acyclic (aliphatic) compounds have straight or branched open chains.\nEthane, isobutane, acetaldehyde, acetic acid and acetone all have open chains. That makes 5.\nCyclopropane and tetrahydrofuran are alicyclic, so they are excluded.\nBenzene is aromatic, so it is also excluded.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":71,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In the soap industry, glycerol is recovered from spent-lye. Which purification technique is used for this?","image":"","options":["Steam distillation","Distillation under reduced pressure","Fractional distillation","Sublimation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: liquids with very high boiling points that decompose at or below their boiling point are purified by distillation under reduced pressure.\nLowering the pressure on the liquid surface lowers its boiling point, so glycerol distils without decomposing.\nNCERT gives glycerol from spent-lye as the example of this method.\nSteam distillation is the tempting choice, but it needs a steam-volatile liquid that is immiscible with water. Glycerol is completely miscible with water.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":72,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"The common name of HCOOH comes from the Latin word for the organism in which the acid was first found. Which organism is this?","image":"","options":["Citrus fruits","Red ants","Vinegar-forming bacteria","Butter"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Trivial (common) names usually reflect the source of a compound.\nHCOOH was first obtained from red ants. The Latin word for ant is 'formica', which gives the name formic acid.\nCitric acid is the tempting wrong choice: it takes its name from citrus fruits, not from ants.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":73,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (molecule) with List-II (number of σ and π bonds).\nList-I:\n(a) $CH_3–CH_3$\n(b) $CH_2=CH_2$\n(c) $HC \\equiv CH$\n(d) $CH_2=C=CH_2$\nList-II:\n(i) 3 σ, 2 π\n(ii) 7 σ, 0 π\n(iii) 6 σ, 2 π\n(iv) 5 σ, 1 π","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(a) Ethane has 6 C–H bonds and 1 C–C bond, all single, giving 7 σ and 0 π, so it matches (ii).\n(b) Ethene has 4 C–H bonds plus one C=C (1 σ + 1 π), giving 5 σ and 1 π, so it matches (iv).\n(c) Ethyne has 2 C–H bonds plus one C≡C (1 σ + 2 π), giving 3 σ and 2 π, so it matches (i).\n(d) Propadiene has 4 C–H bonds plus two C=C bonds, giving 6 σ and 2 π, so it matches (iii).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":74,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For a compound containing both nitrogen and sulphur, fusion with excess sodium gives sodium cyanide and sodium sulphide. The sodium extract then gives Prussian blue instead of a blood-red colour.\nStatement II: Hydrazine ($NH_2NH_2$) gives a positive Lassaigne's test for nitrogen because it contains nitrogen atoms.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. With excess sodium, the NaSCN formed decomposes: NaSCN + 2Na → NaCN + $Na_2S$. The $CN^-$ ions then give Prussian blue, and sulphide gives its own test, rather than the blood-red ferric thiocyanate.\nStatement II: Incorrect. Lassaigne's test needs nitrogen converted to $CN^-$, which requires carbon. Hydrazine contains no carbon, so no NaCN forms and the test is negative.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":75,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (element estimated) with List-II (form that is finally weighed or measured):\nList-I\n(a) Halogen by Carius method\n(b) Sulphur\n(c) Phosphorus\n(d) Carbon by Liebig's method\nList-II\n(i) BaSO$_4$\n(ii) AgX\n(iii) CO$_2$ absorbed in KOH solution\n(iv) Mg$_2$P$_2$O$_7$","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) In the Carius method, the halogen is precipitated with AgNO$_3$ and weighed as AgX, so (a)-(ii).\n(b) Sulphur is oxidised to H$_2$SO$_4$ and precipitated as BaSO$_4$, so (b)-(i).\n(c) Phosphorus is precipitated and ignited to Mg$_2$P$_2$O$_7$, so (c)-(iv).\n(d) In Liebig's method, CO$_2$ is absorbed in KOH solution and found from the gain in mass, so (d)-(iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":76,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In a Carius determination, 0.25 g of an organic compound gave 0.287 g of silver chloride. What is the percentage of chlorine in the compound? (Atomic masses: Ag = 108, Cl = 35.5)","image":"","options":["28.4%","14.2%","56.8%","35.5%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 0.25 g of compound, $m_1$ = 0.287 g of AgCl, molar mass of AgCl = 108 + 35.5 = 143.5 g/mol.\nFormula: % Cl = $\\frac{35.5 \\times m_1 \\times 100}{143.5 \\times m}$\nSubstituting: % Cl = $\\frac{35.5 \\times 0.287 \\times 100}{143.5 \\times 0.25}$. Here 0.287/143.5 = 0.002 mol of AgCl, which contains 0.071 g of Cl.\nSo % Cl = 0.071 × 100 / 0.25 = 28.4%\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":77,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A heptane chain carrying an ethyl group and a methyl group, which get locants 3 and 5 from either end, is named 3-ethyl-5-methylheptane and not 5-ethyl-3-methylheptane.\nReason (R): When two numbering directions give identical locant sets, the lower number goes to the substituent that comes first in alphabetical order.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Numbering from either end gives the same locant set {3,5}, so the lowest locant rule cannot decide.\nR is true: in such a tie, the substituent that comes first alphabetically gets the lower number.\n'Ethyl' comes before 'methyl', so ethyl gets 3 and the name is 3-ethyl-5-methylheptane. A is true.\nR is exactly the reason for A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":78,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Kjeldahl's method cannot be used to estimate nitrogen in nitrobenzene.\nReason (R): The nitrogen of nitro groups is not converted into ammonium sulphate when the compound is heated with concentrated sulphuric acid.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: Kjeldahl's method does not apply to nitro and azo compounds, or to compounds with nitrogen in a ring such as pyridine.\nR is true: in these compounds, digestion with conc. H$_2$SO$_4$ does not convert the nitrogen into ammonium sulphate.\nThe method measures only the NH$_3$ released from (NH$_4$)$_2$SO$_4$. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":79,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Aniline is a non-benzenoid aromatic compound.\nStatement II: Tropolone is aromatic even though it does not contain a benzene ring.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is false. Aniline is C$_6$H$_5$NH$_2$, which contains a benzene ring. NCERT lists it, along with benzene and naphthalene, as a benzenoid aromatic compound.\nStatement II is true. Tropolone is NCERT's example of a non-benzenoid aromatic compound.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":80,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match the alkyl groups in List-I with their structures in List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Isopropyl\n(B) sec-Butyl\n(C) Isobutyl\n(D) tert-Butyl\nList-II:\n(i) $(CH_3)_3C-$\n(ii) $(CH_3)_2CH-$\n(iii) $CH_3CH_2CH(CH_3)-$\n(iv) $(CH_3)_2CHCH_2-$","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Isopropyl is a three-carbon group attached through its middle carbon: $(CH_3)_2CH-$, which is (ii).\n(B) sec-Butyl is attached through a secondary carbon of the butane chain: $CH_3CH_2CH(CH_3)-$, which is (iii).\n(C) Isobutyl is attached through a $CH_2$ next to an isopropyl unit: $(CH_3)_2CHCH_2-$, which is (iv).\n(D) tert-Butyl is attached through a carbon bonded to three methyl groups: $(CH_3)_3C-$, which is (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":81,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Phenol carrying a nitro group para to the –OH group is named 4-nitrophenol.\nReason (R): When a substituent gives the benzene derivative a special parent name (such as phenol or aniline), the ring carbon bearing that substituent is numbered 1.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: in a derivative named phenol, the carbon bearing $-OH$ is C-1. The same applies to aniline ($-NH_2$) and benzoic acid ($-COOH$).\nA is true: with OH at C-1, the para position is C-4, so the name is 4-nitrophenol (common name p-nitrophenol).\nR directly fixes this numbering, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":82,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In paper chromatography, what acts as the stationary phase?","image":"","options":["The cellulose fibres acting as an adsorbent","Water trapped in the chromatography paper","The organic solvent rising up the paper","A thin layer of alumina coated on the paper"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Paper chromatography is a type of partition chromatography.\nThe special chromatography paper holds water within it, and this trapped water is the stationary phase.\nThe solvent that rises up the paper is the mobile phase.\nComponents separate because they partition differently between these two liquid phases.\nThe tempting option (1) is wrong because separation here is not by adsorption on cellulose.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":83,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about stereoisomers is correct?","image":"","options":["They have different molecular formulas but the same structural formula","They have the same constitution and sequence of covalent bonds but differ in the relative arrangement of atoms in space","They differ in the functional group present","They differ in the length of the main carbon chain"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Stereoisomers have the same molecular formula and the same constitution, meaning the atoms are bonded in the same sequence.\nThey differ only in how the atoms or groups are arranged in space.\nGeometrical and optical isomerism are the two types.\nOptions (3) and (4) describe structural isomers, such as functional group and chain isomers.\nOption (1) is wrong because all isomers share the same molecular formula.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":84,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Trichloroacetic acid is a stronger acid than acetic acid.\nReason (R): The inductive effect becomes stronger as the distance from the substituent increases.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Three Cl atoms withdraw electron density by the –I effect. This disperses the negative charge on the carboxylate ion and stabilises it, so the acid ionises more easily.\nR is false. The inductive effect weakens rapidly with distance and becomes negligible after about three bonds.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":85,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In $(CH_3)_3C^+$, nine α C–H bonds can take part in hyperconjugation.\n(ii) $CH_3^+$ is stabilised by hyperconjugation.\n(iii) The positively charged carbon of a carbocation is $sp^2$ hybridised and has a vacant unhybridised p orbital perpendicular to the molecular plane.\n(iv) In the methyl carbanion, carbon is $sp^3$ hybridised and the ion is pyramidal.\n(v) The stability order is $CH_3^+ > CH_3CH_2^+ > (CH_3)_2CH^+ > (CH_3)_3C^+$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: three methyl groups provide 9 α C–H bonds.\n(ii) Incorrect: $CH_3^+$ has no α C–H bond, so hyperconjugation is not possible.\n(iii) Correct: the carbocation is trigonal planar with an empty p orbital.\n(iv) Correct: the carbanion is $sp^3$ hybridised, with the lone pair in one hybrid orbital, giving a pyramidal shape.\n(v) Incorrect: the true order is the reverse, $3^\\circ > 2^\\circ > 1^\\circ > CH_3^+$.\nCorrect statements = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":86,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The actual structure of the acetate ion is more stable than either of its canonical structures.\nReason (R): The two canonical structures of the acetate ion are real species that interconvert rapidly.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The resonance hybrid of the acetate ion has lower energy than any of its contributing structures. The negative charge is delocalised equally over both oxygen atoms, which gives resonance stabilisation.\nR is false. NCERT states that canonical structures are hypothetical and individually do not represent any real molecule.\nThe molecule does not flip back and forth between them; only the single hybrid exists.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":87,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about resonance are correct?\n(i) All canonical structures must have the same positions of nuclei.\n(ii) All canonical structures must have the same number of unpaired electrons.\n(iii) A canonical structure with greater separation of opposite charges contributes more to the hybrid.\n(iv) Resonance energy is the difference in energy between the actual structure and the lowest-energy canonical structure.\n(v) Among canonical structures, one with more covalent bonds is generally more stable.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: only electrons move between canonical forms; the nuclei stay fixed.\n(ii) Correct: all canonical forms must have the same number of unpaired electrons.\n(iii) Incorrect: less charge separation gives a more stable, more contributing structure.\n(iv) Correct: this is how NCERT defines resonance energy.\n(v) Correct: more covalent bonds usually means greater stability.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":88,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match the group in List-I with the prefix used for it in IUPAC names (List-II), when the group is not the principal functional group.\nList-I:\n(A) –CN\n(B) –OH\n(C) –NH$_2$\n(D) >C=O (ketone)\nList-II:\n(i) amino\n(ii) oxo\n(iii) cyano\n(iv) hydroxy","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Each pair:\n(A) –CN is written as cyano (iii).\n(B) –OH is written as hydroxy (iv).\n(C) –NH$_2$ is written as amino (i).\n(D) A ketonic >C=O is written as oxo (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":89,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about bonding in hydrocarbons is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The π electron cloud lies above and below the plane of the bonded atoms, so π bonds act as reactive centres in molecules with multiple bonds.","The C–H bond in ethane is shorter than the C–H bond in ethyne, because an $sp^3$ orbital is more compact than an $sp$ orbital.","An $sp$ hybridised carbon attracts the electrons of a C–H bond more strongly than an $sp^3$ hybridised carbon does.","In ethene, one $CH_2$ group cannot be twisted relative to the other without destroying the sideways overlap of the p orbitals."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: π electrons are loosely held and exposed, so attacking reagents can reach them easily.\n(2) Incorrect: an $sp$ orbital has the most s-character and is the most compact. The C–H bond in ethyne is therefore the shortest, and C–H bond length decreases in the order ethane > ethene > ethyne.\n(3) Correct: higher s-character makes the carbon more electronegative.\n(4) Correct: a π bond needs the p orbitals to stay parallel, so rotation about C=C is restricted.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":90,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the bond-line formula of the compound whose condensed formula is $HOCH_2CH_2CH_2CH(CH_3)CH(CH_3)CH_3$. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Exactly three line-ends in the bond-line formula represent $CH_3$ groups.\n(b) The total number of hydrogen atoms attached to carbon (which are not drawn) is 17.\n(c) The oxygen atom and the hydrogen atom attached to it are written explicitly as OH.\n(d) The carbon skeleton contains a total of 7 carbon atoms.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Expanded skeleton: HO–C1–C2–C3–C4(CH3)–C5(CH3)–C6, giving 8 carbons. So (d) is incorrect.\n(a) Correct: the $CH_3$ groups are the branch on C4, the branch on C5, and the terminal C6, making 3 line-ends.\n(b) Correct: H on carbon = 3×2 (CH2) + 1 + 1 (CH) + 3×3 (CH3) = 17.\n(c) Correct: heteroatoms and the H on them are always written in bond-line formulas.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":91,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following groups can appear in an IUPAC name only as a prefix (never as a suffix)?\n–Cl, –NO$_2$, –OCH$_3$, –CN, –OH, –Br, –NH$_2$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Judge each group:\n• –Cl (chloro), –Br (bromo), –NO$_2$ (nitro) and –OCH$_3$ (methoxy) are prefix-only.\n• –CN can be a suffix: -nitrile.\n• –OH can be a suffix: -ol.\n• –NH$_2$ can be a suffix: -amine.\nThat makes 4 prefix-only groups.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":92,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A benzene ring with –COOH and –SO$_3$H groups para to each other is named 4-sulfobenzoic acid.\nReason (R): –COOH has higher priority than –SO$_3$H, so the ring is named as benzoic acid, with the –COOH-bearing carbon as C-1.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. In the NCERT priority order, –COOH comes first, ahead of –SO$_3$H.\nBecause –COOH is the principal group, the parent is benzoic acid, and the carbon carrying –COOH is C-1.\nThe para –SO$_3$H group is then at C-4 and is named with the prefix sulfo.\nSo A is true, and R correctly explains it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":93,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: $C_4H_{10}$ exists as two chain isomers, butane and 2-methylpropane.\nStatement II: Propane ($C_3H_8$) also shows chain isomerism.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Four carbons can form a straight chain (butane) or a branched chain (2-methylpropane).\nStatement II is incorrect. Three carbons can only be joined in one way, $CH_3CH_2CH_3$.\nAny 'branched' drawing of propane is the same molecule redrawn.\nChain isomerism among alkanes therefore begins at $C_4$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":94,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is a correct condensed structural formula of n-hexane?","image":"","options":["$CH_3(CH_2)_6CH_3$","$CH_3(CH_2)_4CH_3$","$CH_3(CH_2)_3CH_3$","$(CH_3)_2(CH_2)_4CH_3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"n-Hexane is an unbranched chain of 6 carbons: $CH_3CH_2CH_2CH_2CH_2CH_3$.\nIn a condensed formula, identical repeating groups are grouped together with a subscript.\nThe chain has two terminal $CH_3$ groups and four $CH_2$ groups, so the formula is $CH_3(CH_2)_4CH_3$.\nOption (1) has 8 carbons (octane). Option (3) has 5 carbons (pentane).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":95,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The correct IUPAC name of a decane carrying a sec-butyl group at C-5 and an isopropyl group at C-4 is written as 5-sec-butyl-4-isopropyldecane.\nReason (R): For alphabetical ordering of substituents, the prefix sec- is ignored, but the prefix iso- is treated as part of the alkyl group's name.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. In alphabetical listing, iso- and neo- count as part of the substituent name, whereas sec- and tert- are disregarded.\nA is true. sec-Butyl is alphabetised under 'b' (butyl), and isopropyl is alphabetised under 'i'. Since b comes before i, sec-butyl is written first, giving 5-sec-butyl-4-isopropyldecane.\nR directly explains why sec-butyl precedes isopropyl in the name.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":96,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The tert-butyl cation, $(CH_3)_3C^+$, is more stable than the methyl cation, $CH_3^+$.\nReason (R): In $(CH_3)_3C^+$, the positively charged carbon is $sp^3$ hybridised, which allows better dispersal of the positive charge.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Carbocation stability follows 3° > 2° > 1° > methyl.\nThe three methyl groups disperse the positive charge through the +I (inductive) effect and through hyperconjugation.\nR is false. Every carbocation carbon, including the tert-butyl cation, is $sp^2$ hybridised and planar, with a vacant p orbital.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":97,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is a heterocyclic aromatic compound in which the ring heteroatom is nitrogen?","image":"","options":["Furan","Thiophene","Pyridine","Tetrahydrofuran"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists furan, thiophene and pyridine as heterocyclic aromatic compounds.\nThe heteroatom differs in each: furan has O, thiophene has S and pyridine has N.\nTetrahydrofuran also contains O in its ring, but it is a saturated alicyclic heterocycle and is not aromatic.\nSo pyridine is the only option that is both aromatic and nitrogen-containing.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":98,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) $R_f$ = distance moved by the substance from the base line ÷ distance moved by the solvent from the base line.\n(ii) In column (adsorption) chromatography, the component adsorbed most strongly is eluted first.\n(iii) Paper chromatography is a type of partition chromatography in which water held in the paper acts as the stationary phase.\n(iv) Colourless amino acid spots on a TLC plate can be made visible by spraying with ninhydrin.\n(v) Spots of compounds that fluoresce can be detected on a TLC plate under ultraviolet light.\n(vi) In differential extraction, the organic solvent chosen must be miscible with water.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is the definition of the retardation factor.\n(ii) Incorrect: the strongly adsorbed component stays near the top. Weakly adsorbed ones move down and are eluted first.\n(iii) Correct: water trapped in the paper is the stationary phase.\n(iv) Correct: ninhydrin reveals amino acids.\n(v) Correct: UV light is used to see fluorescent spots.\n(vi) Incorrect: the solvent must be immiscible with water so that two layers form.\nCorrect statements = 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":99,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following species can act as a nucleophile?","image":"","options":["$NO_2^+$","$(CH_3)_3C^+$","$(CH_3)_3N$","$BF_3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A nucleophile donates an electron pair (it is 'nucleus seeking').\nIn trimethylamine, nitrogen carries a lone pair, so this neutral molecule can act as a nucleophile.\n$NO_2^+$ and $(CH_3)_3C^+$ are electron-deficient cations, so they are electrophiles.\n$BF_3$ has an incomplete octet on boron, so it accepts an electron pair and is also an electrophile.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":100,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"An unbranched nonane chain carries a tert-butyl group on C-4 and an isopropyl group on C-5. This numbering already gives the lowest locants. Which is the correctly written IUPAC name?","image":"","options":["5-Isopropyl-4-tert-butylnonane","4-Isopropyl-5-tert-butylnonane","4-tert-Butyl-5-isopropylnonane","6-tert-Butyl-5-isopropylnonane"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Locants: numbering from one end gives {4,5}; from the other end it gives {5,6}. So {4,5} is the correct set, which rules out option (4).\nAlphabetical order: tert- is ignored, so tert-butyl is filed under 'b'. Iso- is counted, so isopropyl is filed under 'i'.\nBecause 'b' comes before 'i', butyl is written first.\nThe correct name is 4-tert-butyl-5-isopropylnonane.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":101,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In heterolytic cleavage of $CH_3–Z$, a carbanion is formed when Z is less electronegative than carbon.\n(ii) Alkyl free radicals follow the stability order: tertiary > secondary > primary > methyl.\n(iii) Homolytic cleavage generally needs energy, supplied by heat or light, or is initiated by peroxides.\n(iv) A carbocation has a vacant $sp^2$ hybrid orbital perpendicular to the plane of its bonds.\n(v) Carbocations are highly unstable and reactive species.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: a less electronegative Z leaves without the bonding pair, so carbon keeps the pair and becomes $CH_3^-$.\n(ii) Correct: alkyl groups stabilise radicals, giving 3° > 2° > 1° > methyl.\n(iii) Correct: homolysis is promoted by heat, light or peroxides.\n(iv) Incorrect: the vacant orbital is the unhybridised p orbital, not an $sp^2$ orbital.\n(v) Correct: carbocations are reactive intermediates.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":102,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In a Dumas determination, 0.40 g of an organic compound gave 56 mL of moist nitrogen, collected over water at 300 K and a total pressure of 750 mm Hg. The aqueous tension at 300 K is 26 mm Hg. What is the percentage of nitrogen in the compound? (Atomic mass of N = 14; 1 mol of gas occupies 22400 mL at STP, 273 K and 760 mm Hg)","image":"","options":["15.17%","15.72%","17.50%","7.59%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $V_1$ = 56 mL, $T_1$ = 300 K, pressure of dry $N_2$ = 750 − 26 = 724 mm Hg.\nVolume at STP: V = $\\frac{724 \\times 56 \\times 273}{760 \\times 300}$ ≈ 48.55 mL\nMass of $N_2$ = $\\frac{28 \\times 48.55}{22400}$ ≈ 0.0607 g\n% N = 0.0607 × 100/0.40 ≈ 15.17%\nOption (2), 15.72%, results from forgetting to subtract the aqueous tension.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":103,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"What is the hybridisation of each carbon atom in ethyne ($HC \\equiv CH$), and what is the H–C–C bond angle?","image":"","options":["$sp^3$, 109.5°","$sp^2$, 120°","$sp$, 180°","$sp^2$, 180°"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Each carbon in ethyne forms two σ bonds (one C–H and one C–C) and two π bonds.\nTwo σ bonds need two hybrid orbitals, so the carbon is $sp$ hybridised.\nThe two $sp$ orbitals point in opposite directions, so the molecule is linear with a 180° angle.\n$sp^2$ carbons are trigonal planar (120°), so a 180° angle cannot go with $sp^2$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":104,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (separation task) with List-II (most suitable technique):\nList-I\n(a) Recovering glycerol from spent-lye in the soap industry\n(b) Separating aniline from an aniline–water mixture\n(c) Separating fractions of crude oil\n(d) Purifying camphor from non-volatile impurities\nList-II\n(i) Sublimation\n(ii) Fractional distillation\n(iii) Distillation under reduced pressure\n(iv) Steam distillation","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Glycerol decomposes at its normal boiling point, so it is distilled under reduced pressure: (iii).\n(b) Aniline is steam-volatile and immiscible with water, so steam distillation is used: (iv).\n(c) Crude oil components have close boiling points, so fractional distillation is used: (ii).\n(d) Camphor sublimes, so sublimation purifies it: (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":105,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"What is the IUPAC name of CH$_3$–CH(OH)–CH$_2$–COOH?","image":"","options":["2-hydroxybutanoic acid","3-hydroxybutanoic acid","4-hydroxybutanoic acid","3-carboxypropan-2-ol"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"–COOH outranks –OH, so the compound is named as a butanoic acid.\nThe chain has four carbons, and the –COOH carbon is C-1.\nCounting from it: CH$_2$ is C-2 and CH(OH) is C-3.\n–OH therefore becomes the prefix 3-hydroxy, giving 3-hydroxybutanoic acid.\nThe name '3-carboxypropan-2-ol' wrongly treats –OH as the principal group.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":106,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following acids in decreasing order of acid strength:\n(I) $FCH_2COOH$\n(II) $ClCH_2COOH$\n(III) $BrCH_2COOH$\n(IV) $CH_3COOH$","image":"","options":["I > II > III > IV","III > II > I > IV","IV > III > II > I","II > I > III > IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A –I halogen withdraws electron density from the carboxylate ion and so stabilises it. Greater stabilisation means a stronger acid.\nThe strength of the –I effect follows electronegativity: F > Cl > Br.\nThe methyl group in $CH_3COOH$ is +I, which destabilises the anion, so acetic acid is the weakest.\nThe order is therefore $FCH_2COOH > ClCH_2COOH > BrCH_2COOH > CH_3COOH$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":107,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In steam distillation, an organic liquid immiscible with water distils at a temperature below 373 K at atmospheric pressure.\nReason (R): The mixture boils when the sum of the vapour pressures of the organic liquid and of water becomes equal to the atmospheric pressure.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: for the immiscible mixture, the total pressure is $p = p_1 + p_2$. Boiling occurs when $p$ equals atmospheric pressure.\nA is true: water alone would need $p_2$ = 1 atm to boil.\nBecause the organic liquid contributes $p_1$, the required $p_2$ is less than 1 atm. So boiling starts below 373 K, which is also below the organic liquid's own boiling point.\nR therefore directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":108,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Consider propadiene (allene), $H_2C=C=CH_2$. Which statement about its bonding and shape is correct?","image":"","options":["The central carbon is $sp$ and the terminal carbons are $sp^2$. The two π bonds lie in mutually perpendicular planes, so the two terminal $CH_2$ groups lie in perpendicular planes.","All three carbons are $sp^2$ hybridised, and all seven atoms lie in one plane.","The central carbon is $sp^2$ hybridised, and the C=C=C bond angle is 120°.","The central carbon is $sp$. Both π bonds use p orbitals parallel to each other, so the molecule is planar."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The central carbon forms two σ bonds, so it is $sp$ hybridised and C=C=C is linear (180°).\nIt has two unhybridised p orbitals left over, and these are perpendicular to each other.\nEach terminal carbon forms three σ bonds, so it is $sp^2$. Each π bond requires its p orbitals to be parallel.\nOne π bond therefore lies in one plane and the other in a perpendicular plane. The two $CH_2$ groups follow these planes, so they lie in perpendicular planes and the molecule is not planar.\nThis rules out options 2, 3 and 4.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":109,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Camphor mixed with sodium chloride can be purified by sublimation.\nReason (R): Some solids change directly from the solid to the vapour state on heating, without passing through the liquid state.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Camphor sublimes on heating, but NaCl does not. Collecting the vapour as a solid separates the two.\nR is true. Sublimation is the direct conversion of a solid into vapour on heating.\nR is the correct explanation of A, because sublimation separates a sublimable compound from a non-sublimable impurity.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":110,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following species act as electrophiles?\n$CH_3^+$, $NO_2^+$, $H_2O$, $OH^-$, $(CH_3)_3N$, $CH_3^-$","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An electrophile is electron-seeking, so it is electron deficient. A nucleophile donates an electron pair.\n$CH_3^+$: carbocation, so it is an electrophile.\n$NO_2^+$: electron-deficient cation, so it is an electrophile.\n$H_2O$ and $(CH_3)_3N$: have lone pairs, so they are nucleophiles.\n$OH^-$ and $CH_3^-$: anions with electron pairs, so they are nucleophiles.\nTotal electrophiles = 2.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":111,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (reaction) with List-II (type of reaction):\nList-I\n(A) $CH_3CH_2Br + OH^- \\rightarrow CH_3CH_2OH + Br^-$\n(B) $CH_2=CH_2 + Br_2 \\rightarrow BrCH_2CH_2Br$\n(C) $CH_3CH_2Br + KOH(alc) \\rightarrow CH_2=CH_2 + KBr + H_2O$\n(D) $NH_4CNO \\xrightarrow{heat} NH_2CONH_2$\nList-II\n(I) Elimination\n(II) Rearrangement\n(III) Substitution\n(IV) Addition","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) OH⁻ replaces Br, so this is substitution (III).\n(B) Two Br atoms add across the C=C double bond, so this is addition (IV).\n(C) H and Br are removed from adjacent carbons, forming a C=C bond, so this is elimination (I).\n(D) Wöhler's conversion of ammonium cyanate to urea reorganises the same atoms into a new structure, so this is rearrangement (II).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":112,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"A benzene ring carries three substituents: a $-CHO$ group, an $-OH$ group on the carbon adjacent (ortho) to the $-CHO$ carbon, and a $-NO_2$ group on the carbon para to the $-OH$ group. What is its correct IUPAC name?","image":"","options":["4-Nitro-2-formylphenol","6-Hydroxy-3-nitrobenzaldehyde","5-Nitro-2-hydroxybenzaldehyde","2-Hydroxy-5-nitrobenzaldehyde"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Step 1: Pick the parent. $-CHO$ outranks $-OH$, so the parent is benzaldehyde and the CHO carbon is C-1. This rules out names based on phenol.\nStep 2: Number the ring. Going towards OH places OH at C-2 and $NO_2$ at C-5, giving {2,5}. Going the other way gives {3,6}. Since 2 < 3, {2,5} is the lowest set.\nStep 3: Order the prefixes. They are cited alphabetically, so hydroxy comes before nitro.\nThe name is 2-hydroxy-5-nitrobenzaldehyde.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":113,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many structural isomers are possible for the alkane $C_5H_{12}$?","image":"","options":["2","4","3","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"These isomers differ only in the carbon skeleton, so they are chain isomers.\n(i) Straight chain: pentane, $CH_3(CH_2)_3CH_3$.\n(ii) One branch: 2-methylbutane (isopentane).\n(iii) Two branches on one carbon: 2,2-dimethylpropane (neopentane).\nNo other distinct skeleton is possible. For example, '3-methylbutane' is just 2-methylbutane numbered wrongly.\nSo there are 3 isomers.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":114,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"What is the correct IUPAC name of HOOC–CH$_2$–CH(CN)–CH$_2$–CHO?","image":"","options":["3-cyano-4-formylbutanoic acid","3-cyano-5-oxopentanoic acid","4-carboxy-3-cyanobutanal","5-oxo-3-cyanopentanoic acid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"–COOH has the highest priority, so the compound is named as an acid, and its carbon is C-1.\nThe longest chain containing –COOH also includes the –CHO carbon. That gives five carbons: pentanoic acid.\nBecause the –CHO carbon is inside the chain, it is shown as 5-oxo. 'Formyl' is used only when the CHO carbon lies outside the chain.\n–CN is on C-3 and becomes 3-cyano.\nIn alphabetical order, cyano comes before oxo: 3-cyano-5-oxopentanoic acid.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":115,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Both carbon–oxygen bonds in the acetate ion, $CH_3COO^-$, have the same length.\nReason (R): The actual structure of the acetate ion is a resonance hybrid of two equivalent canonical structures.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Experimentally, the two C–O bonds in acetate are of equal length, intermediate between a C–O single bond and a C=O double bond.\nR is true. The negative charge and the double bond are delocalised over both oxygens. The real ion is the hybrid of two equivalent contributing structures.\nBecause the two canonical forms contribute equally, the two bonds become identical. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":116,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"The phosphoric acid obtained by oxidising 0.50 g of an organic compound was precipitated as MgNH$_4$PO$_4$. On ignition, this gave 0.444 g of Mg$_2$P$_2$O$_7$. What is the percentage of phosphorus in the compound? (Atomic masses: Mg = 24, P = 31, O = 16)","image":"","options":["24.8%","12.4%","49.6%","27.9%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 0.50 g, m (Mg$_2$P$_2$O$_7$) = 0.444 g, molar mass = 222 g/mol. One formula unit contains 2 P, that is 62 g of P.\nFormula: % P = $\\frac{62 \\times m_1 \\times 100}{222 \\times m}$\nSubstituting: mass of P = $\\frac{62 \\times 0.444}{222}$ = 0.124 g\n% P = $\\frac{0.124}{0.50} \\times 100$ = 24.8%\nUsing 31 in place of 62 gives the wrong value 12.4%.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":117,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"On a developed TLC plate, the solvent front is 12.0 cm above the base line. The centre of the spot of compound X is 4.5 cm above the base line. What is the $R_f$ value of X?","image":"","options":["0.375","2.67","0.625","0.45"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: distance moved by X = 4.5 cm; distance moved by solvent = 12.0 cm (both measured from the base line).\nFormula: $R_f = \\frac{\\text{distance moved by substance}}{\\text{distance moved by solvent}}$\nSubstituting: $R_f = \\frac{4.5}{12.0}$\nSo $R_f$ = 0.375. It must be less than 1, which rules out 2.67.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":118,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (electronic effect) with List-II (its feature):\nList-I\n(a) Inductive effect\n(b) Electromeric effect\n(c) Hyperconjugation\n(d) Resonance effect\nList-II\n(i) Temporary effect; operates on a multiple bond only in the presence of an attacking reagent\n(ii) Permanent polarisation transmitted through σ bonds\n(iii) Delocalisation of σ electrons of a C–H bond into an adjacent unsaturated system or vacant p orbital\n(iv) Polarity produced by interaction of π electrons (or lone pairs) in a conjugated system","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Inductive effect: a permanent shift of σ electrons, so (ii).\n(b) Electromeric effect: a temporary, complete transfer of π electrons, occurring only when a reagent attacks, so (i).\n(c) Hyperconjugation: involves α C–H σ electrons and is also called no-bond resonance, so (iii).\n(d) Resonance effect: arises from conjugated π electrons or lone pairs, so (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":119,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In curved-arrow notation, a half-headed ('fish-hook') arrow is used to show:","image":"","options":["movement of a pair of electrons","movement of a single electron","migration of a proton","transfer of a positive charge to an atom"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A full-headed curved arrow shows the movement of an electron pair.\nA half-headed (fish-hook) arrow shows the movement of one electron. It is used in homolytic cleavage, where each atom keeps one electron of the shared pair.\nProton or charge transfer is not shown with a special arrow type.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":120,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Tropolone is aromatic even though its ring is not a benzene ring. According to NCERT's classification, tropolone is a:","image":"","options":["Benzenoid aromatic compound","Non-benzenoid aromatic compound","Alicyclic homocyclic compound","Heterocyclic aromatic compound"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT divides aromatic compounds into benzenoid compounds, which contain a benzene ring, and non-benzenoid compounds, which are aromatic without a benzene ring.\nNCERT gives tropolone as its example of a non-benzenoid aromatic compound.\nIts seven-membered ring is made only of carbon atoms, so it is not heterocyclic.\nIt shows aromatic character, so it is not merely alicyclic.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":121,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many isomers in total, including cyclic structures and geometrical isomers, are possible for $C_4H_8$?","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Open-chain alkenes:\n- But-1-ene\n- But-2-ene, which exists as cis and trans forms (2)\n- 2-methylpropene\nThat makes 4 open-chain isomers.\nBut-1-ene and 2-methylpropene cannot show geometrical isomerism, because one double-bond carbon carries two identical H atoms or two identical $CH_3$ groups.\nCyclic isomers: cyclobutane and methylcyclopropane, which are functional (ring–chain) isomers of the alkenes. That adds 2.\nTotal = 4 + 2 = 6.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":122,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Removing one hydrogen atom from the central carbon atom of propane gives an alkyl group. What is this group called?","image":"","options":["n-Propyl","Isopropyl","sec-Butyl","Ethyl"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An alkyl group forms when one H is removed from a carbon of an alkane.\nRemoving H from a terminal carbon of propane gives $CH_3CH_2CH_2$– (n-propyl).\nRemoving H from the central carbon gives $(CH_3)_2CH$–, which is the isopropyl group.\nsec-Butyl has four carbons, so it cannot come from propane.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":123,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following species can act as nucleophiles?\n$OH^-$, $NH_3$, $AlCl_3$, $CN^-$, $NO_2^+$, $H_2O$, $(CH_3)_3C^+$, $Br^+$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A nucleophile donates an electron pair, so it must have an unshared pair or a negative charge.\nNucleophiles: $OH^-$, $CN^-$ (negative charge with a lone pair) and $NH_3$, $H_2O$ (neutral, with lone pairs). That makes 4.\nElectrophiles: $AlCl_3$ (electron-deficient Al), $NO_2^+$, $(CH_3)_3C^+$ and $Br^+$ (positive, electron-seeking).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":124,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In the detection of carbon and hydrogen, an organic compound is heated with copper(II) oxide. The gases evolved are passed first over anhydrous copper sulphate and then through lime water. Which observation shows that hydrogen is present in the compound?","image":"","options":["Lime water turns milky","White anhydrous copper sulphate turns blue","Black copper(II) oxide turns blue","A black precipitate forms in lime water"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Copper(II) oxide oxidises carbon to $CO_2$ and hydrogen to $H_2O$.\nThe water vapour hydrates white anhydrous $CuSO_4$ to blue $CuSO_4\\cdot5H_2O$. This colour change shows hydrogen.\nLime water turning milky ($CaCO_3$ forms) shows carbon, not hydrogen. That makes it the tempting wrong option.\nCuO is reduced to Cu, so it does not turn blue. Lime water never gives a black precipitate.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":125,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following acids:\n(I) $CH_3CH_2CHClCOOH$\n(II) $CH_3CHClCH_2COOH$\n(III) $ClCH_2CH_2CH_2COOH$\n(IV) $CH_3CH_2CH_2COOH$\nThe correct order of decreasing acid strength is:","image":"","options":["III > II > I > IV","I > II > III > IV","I > III > II > IV","IV > III > II > I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"All three chloro acids are stronger than butanoic acid (IV), because the –I effect of Cl stabilises the carboxylate ion.\nThe inductive effect decreases rapidly with distance and is almost negligible after about three bonds.\nIn (I), Cl is on the α-carbon, closest to the –COOH group, so its effect is strongest.\nIn (II), Cl is on the β-carbon, so the effect is weaker.\nIn (III), Cl is on the γ-carbon, so the effect is weaker still.\nThe order is I > II > III > IV.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":126,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"What is the correct IUPAC name of $CH_3CH_2CH(CH_2CH_3)CH_2CH_2CH(CH_3)CH_2CH_3$?","image":"","options":["6-Ethyl-3-methyloctane","3-Methyl-6-ethyloctane","3-Ethyl-6-methyloctane","3,6-Dimethylnonane"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The longest chain has 8 carbons, so the parent is octane. Going through the ethyl branch does not give a longer chain.\nNumbering from the left puts ethyl at C-3 and methyl at C-6. Numbering from the right puts methyl at C-3 and ethyl at C-6. Both directions give the locant set {3,6}.\nWhen locants tie, the substituent that comes first alphabetically (ethyl) gets the lower number.\nSubstituents are written alphabetically, so the name is 3-ethyl-6-methyloctane.\nOption (2) lists the substituents out of alphabetical order. Option (4) assumes a 9-carbon chain that does not exist.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":127,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Pentane and 2,2-dimethylpropane are chain isomers.\nReason (R): Chain isomers have the same carbon skeleton but differ in the position of the functional group.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Both compounds are $C_5H_{12}$, but one has a straight chain and the other is highly branched.\nR is false. Chain isomers differ in the carbon skeleton itself.\nA same skeleton with a functional group in a different position is the definition of position isomerism.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":128,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The carbon–carbon bond in ethyne is shorter than the carbon–carbon bond in ethane.\nReason (R): An $sp$ hybrid orbital has less s-character than an $sp^3$ hybrid orbital.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The triple bond in ethyne joins $sp$ carbons, and it is shorter and stronger than the single bond between $sp^3$ carbons in ethane.\nR is false. An $sp$ orbital has 50% s-character, while an $sp^3$ orbital has only 25%.\nThe true reason for A is the higher s-character of $sp$ orbitals together with the extra π bonds.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":129,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"During purification by crystallisation, the hot solution of an impure compound is often found to be coloured. Which substance is added to remove the colouring impurities?","image":"","options":["Anhydrous sodium sulphate","Silica gel","Activated charcoal","Iodine crystals"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In crystallisation, the impure compound is dissolved in a hot solvent in which it is only sparingly soluble at room temperature.\nAny colouring impurities are removed by adding activated charcoal, which adsorbs them.\nThe charcoal is then filtered off, and pure crystals form when the filtrate cools.\nSilica gel is an adsorbent used in chromatography, not in crystallisation.\nIodine crystals are used to make spots visible on TLC plates.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":130,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: An organic compound containing both nitrogen and sulphur, when fused with excess sodium, may give a Prussian blue colour in the nitrogen test instead of a blood-red colour with iron(III) ions.\nStatement II: With excess sodium, sodium thiocyanate decomposes to give sodium cyanide and sodium sulphide.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"With a limited amount of sodium, N and S together form $NaSCN$. This gives blood-red $[Fe(SCN)]^{2+}$ with $Fe^{3+}$, and no Prussian blue appears because there is no free $CN^-$.\nStatement II is correct: excess sodium decomposes the thiocyanate, $NaSCN + 2Na \\rightarrow NaCN + Na_2S$.\nStatement I is correct: the $CN^-$ released now gives Prussian blue, and the $S^{2-}$ responds to the separate sulphur tests.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":131,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (pair of compounds) with List-II (type of isomerism):\nList-I\n(A) $CH_3CH_2CH_2CH_2CH_3$ and $C(CH_3)_4$\n(B) $CH_3CH_2OCH_2CH_3$ and $CH_3OCH_2CH_2CH_3$\n(C) $CH_3CH_2CH_2OH$ and $CH_3OCH_2CH_3$\n(D) $CH_3CH_2CH_2Cl$ and $CH_3CHClCH_3$\nList-II\n(I) Position isomerism\n(II) Functional group isomerism\n(III) Metamerism\n(IV) Chain isomerism","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Pentane and neopentane differ in the carbon skeleton, so this is chain isomerism (IV).\n(B) Both are ethers, but the alkyl groups on either side of –O– differ, so these are metamers (III).\n(C) One is an alcohol and the other an ether, both $C_3H_8O$, so this is functional group isomerism (II).\n(D) Cl sits on C-1 in one and on C-2 in the other, so this is position isomerism (I).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":132,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In propadiene ($CH_2=C=CH_2$), the central carbon atom is sp hybridised.\nStatement II: An sp hybridised carbon is more electronegative than an $sp^2$ or $sp^3$ hybridised carbon because its hybrid orbital has a greater s character.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: The central carbon forms two double bonds (two σ and two π bonds). It needs only two hybrid orbitals, so it is sp hybridised. Correct.\nStatement II: An sp orbital has 50% s character, $sp^2$ has 33% and $sp^3$ has 25%. Greater s character keeps electrons closer to the nucleus, so the sp carbon is the most electronegative. Correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":133,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is classified in NCERT as a non-benzenoid aromatic compound?","image":"","options":["Naphthalene","Tropolone","Cyclohexane","Benzaldehyde"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Aromatic compounds are of two types. Benzenoid aromatics contain a benzene ring. Non-benzenoid aromatics are aromatic but have no benzene ring.\nTropolone has a seven-membered ring and no benzene ring, yet it is aromatic. So it is non-benzenoid aromatic.\nNaphthalene has fused benzene rings, so it is benzenoid. Benzaldehyde is a substituted benzene, so it is also benzenoid.\nCyclohexane is not aromatic at all. It is an alicyclic compound.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":134,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (electron displacement effect) with List-II (its description):\nList-I\n(A) Inductive effect\n(B) Resonance effect\n(C) Electromeric effect\n(D) Hyperconjugation\nList-II\n(i) Temporary effect that operates only in the presence of an attacking reagent\n(ii) Permanent polarisation of σ bonds caused by electronegativity difference, transmitted along the chain\n(iii) Delocalisation of C–H σ-electrons into an adjacent empty p orbital or π bond\n(iv) Permanent polarity produced by interaction of π-electrons or lone pairs in a conjugated system","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii","A-iv, B-ii, C-i, D-iii","A-ii, B-iv, C-iii, D-i","A-i, B-iv, C-ii, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The inductive effect is a permanent σ-bond polarisation due to electronegativity difference, so A matches (ii).\n(B) The resonance effect is a permanent polarity arising from π-electrons or lone pairs in conjugation, so B matches (iv).\n(C) The electromeric effect is temporary and appears only when an attacking reagent is present, so C matches (i).\n(D) Hyperconjugation is the delocalisation of C–H σ-electrons, so D matches (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":135,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many carbon atoms in acrylonitrile, $CH_2=CH-C\\equiv N$, are $sp^2$ hybridised?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","0"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"C1 ($CH_2=$) forms one double bond, so it is $sp^2$.\nC2 ($=CH-$) also forms one double bond, so it is $sp^2$.\nC3 ($-C\\equiv N$) forms a triple bond, so it is $sp$.\nThis gives two $sp^2$ carbons. Option (3) would wrongly count the nitrile carbon as $sp^2$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":136,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Organic reactions that proceed through homolytic bond cleavage are known as:","image":"","options":["ionic or heteropolar reactions","free radical or homopolar (nonpolar) reactions","polar substitution reactions","electromeric reactions"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In homolytic cleavage, each bonded atom takes one electron of the shared pair. This produces neutral species with unpaired electrons, called free radicals.\nReactions that proceed this way are called free radical, homopolar or nonpolar reactions.\nIonic, heteropolar or polar reactions involve heterolytic cleavage instead.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":137,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In the common system of naming disubstituted benzenes, the substituents in 1,3-dichlorobenzene are said to be in which relative position?","image":"","options":["ortho (o-)","meta (m-)","para (p-)","ipso"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The common-system terms for disubstituted benzenes are:\n• 1,2-positions = ortho\n• 1,3-positions = meta\n• 1,4-positions = para\nSo 1,3-dichlorobenzene is m-dichlorobenzene.\n'ortho' is tempting, but it applies only to adjacent carbons (1,2).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":138,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following groups releases electrons by the inductive effect (+I effect) when attached to a carbon chain?","image":"","options":["$-NO_2$","$-CH_3$","$-CN$","$-COOH$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Groups that release electrons through a σ-bond chain show the +I effect. NCERT lists alkyl groups such as methyl and ethyl as electron-donating.\nThe groups $-NO_2$, $-CN$ and $-COOH$ are more electronegative or electron-poor. They withdraw electron density, so they show the –I effect.\nSo $-CH_3$ is the only +I group among the options.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":139,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Carbon atoms can bond to one another to form long chains and rings, which is why there are so many organic compounds. What is this property called?","image":"","options":["Catenation","Hybridisation","Resonance","Isomerism"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Catenation is the ability of atoms of an element to link with each other through covalent bonds, forming chains and rings.\nCarbon shows this property to an outstanding extent, which accounts for the huge number of organic compounds.\nHybridisation describes the mixing of orbitals on one atom, not the joining of carbon atoms into chains.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":140,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Both carbon–oxygen bonds in the acetate ion, $CH_3COO^-$, have the same length.\nReason (R): In the acetate ion, the negative charge stays localised on only one of the two oxygen atoms.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The acetate ion is a resonance hybrid of two equivalent canonical structures. Each C–O bond has a length between that of a single and a double bond.\nR is false. Resonance delocalises the negative charge equally over both oxygen atoms.\nIf the charge were localised on one oxygen, the two bond lengths would differ.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":141,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In 1828, Friedrich Wöhler prepared an organic compound from an inorganic one. This weakened the 'vital force' theory. Which conversion did he carry out?","image":"","options":["Ammonium cyanate to urea","Ammonium carbonate to urea","Carbon disulphide to acetic acid","Calcium carbide to acetylene"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: Wöhler heated ammonium cyanate, $NH_4CNO$, and obtained urea, $NH_2CONH_2$.\nWhy it matters: urea is an organic compound, but here it was made from an inorganic salt without any living cell. This went against the vital force theory.\nOption (2) is wrong: urea has the same formula as ammonium cyanate, so this was a rearrangement of the same atoms. Ammonium carbonate was not involved.\nOption (3) is wrong: acetic acid was made from carbon by Kolbe, not Wöhler.\nOption (4) is wrong: methane was made by Berthelot; making acetylene from calcium carbide was not Wöhler's experiment.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":142,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"A compound has several functional groups. Which of the following groups is always written only as a prefix in its IUPAC name and is never used as the principal (suffix) group?","image":"","options":["$-NO_2$","$-OH$","$-CHO$","$-CN$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In IUPAC naming, the group with the highest priority becomes the suffix. All other groups are written as prefixes.\nHalo ($-X$), nitro ($-NO_2$) and alkoxy ($-OR$) groups are always written as prefixes, for example nitro- and chloro-.\n$-OH$ (-ol), $-CHO$ (-al) and $-CN$ (-nitrile) can each act as the suffix when they are the highest-priority group present.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":143,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many structural isomers are possible for the molecular formula $C_3H_8O$?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Alcohols:\n- Propan-1-ol, $CH_3CH_2CH_2OH$\n- Propan-2-ol, $CH_3CH(OH)CH_3$\nEther: methoxyethane, $CH_3OCH_2CH_3$, which is the only possible ether.\nThat gives 2 alcohols + 1 ether = 3 structural isomers.\nThe tempting answer 2 counts only the alcohols and overlooks the functional group isomer.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":144,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a framework model, only the bonds connecting the atoms are shown and the atoms themselves are not shown.\nStatement II: In a space-filling model, atoms are scaled to their van der Waals radii and the bonds are shown as sticks.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. A framework model shows only the bonds and emphasises the pattern of bonding, ignoring atom sizes.\nStatement II is incorrect. A space-filling model does scale atoms to their van der Waals radii, but it shows no bonds at all.\nSticks representing bonds belong to the ball-and-stick model.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":145,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about hyperconjugation are correct?\n(i) It is a permanent effect.\n(ii) The greater the number of alkyl groups attached to a positively charged carbon, the greater the hyperconjugative stabilisation.\n(iii) In propene, σ electrons of the methyl C–H bonds are delocalised into the π orbital of the double bond.\n(iv) In the ethyl cation, the C–C σ bond overlaps with the empty p orbital and provides the hyperconjugative stabilisation.\n(v) The methyl cation $CH_3^+$ cannot be stabilised by hyperconjugation.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: hyperconjugation is a permanent electronic effect.\n(ii) Correct: more alkyl groups provide more α-H atoms, so stabilisation increases. Hence the order is 3° > 2° > 1° carbocation.\n(iii) Correct: NCERT cites propene as an example of hyperconjugation in alkenes.\n(iv) Incorrect: the stabilisation comes from overlap of an α C–H σ bond, not the C–C bond, with the empty p orbital.\n(v) Correct: $CH_3^+$ has no α-hydrogen.\nSo four statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":146,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In the Dumas method, 0.25 g of an organic compound gave 40 mL of nitrogen collected over water at 300 K and 715 mm Hg total pressure. The aqueous tension at 300 K is 15 mm Hg. What is the percentage of nitrogen in the compound? (Take 22400 mL of N$_2$ at STP = 28 g)","image":"","options":["16.8%","17.1%","18.3%","14.0%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: P (dry N$_2$) = 715 − 15 = 700 mm, V = 40 mL, T = 300 K.\nFormula: $V_{STP} = \\frac{P_1 V_1 \\times 273}{760 \\times T_1}$\nSubstituting: $V_{STP} = \\frac{700 \\times 40 \\times 273}{760 \\times 300}$ = 33.53 mL\nMass of N$_2$ = $\\frac{28 \\times 33.53}{22400}$ = 0.0419 g\n% N = $\\frac{0.0419}{0.25} \\times 100$ ≈ 16.8%\nThe value 17.1% results from ignoring the aqueous tension.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":147,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Complete structural formula\n(B) Condensed structural formula\n(C) Bond-line structural formula\n(D) Space-filling model\nList-II\n(i) Carbon and hydrogen atoms are not written; bonds are drawn as zigzag lines\n(ii) Every bond is shown by a dash\n(iii) Groups are written together with some or all bond dashes omitted, e.g. $CH_3(CH_2)_2CH_3$\n(iv) Atom sizes are based on van der Waals radii and bonds are not shown","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) A complete structural formula shows every bond as a dash, so A matches (ii).\n(B) A condensed formula omits dashes and groups identical units, as in $CH_3(CH_2)_2CH_3$, so B matches (iii).\n(C) A bond-line formula is a zigzag of lines with no C or H written, so C matches (i).\n(D) A space-filling model uses van der Waals sizes and shows no bonds, so D matches (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":148,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are correct IUPAC names?\n2-Ethylbutane, 2,2-Dimethylpropane, 3-Methylbutane, 2,3-Dimethylpentane, 4-Methylhexane, Ethylcyclopentane","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"2-Ethylbutane: Incorrect. The longest chain has 5 carbons, so the name is 3-methylpentane.\n2,2-Dimethylpropane: Correct.\n3-Methylbutane: Incorrect. Lower locants are obtained by numbering from the other end, giving 2-methylbutane.\n2,3-Dimethylpentane: Correct.\n4-Methylhexane: Incorrect. It should be 3-methylhexane.\nEthylcyclopentane: Correct. For a single substituent on a ring, no locant is needed.\nSo 3 names are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":149,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"A cyclopentane ring carries two methyl groups on one ring carbon and an ethyl group on the adjacent ring carbon. What is its correct IUPAC name?","image":"","options":["1-Ethyl-2,2-dimethylcyclopentane","2-Ethyl-1,1-dimethylcyclopentane","1,1-Dimethyl-2-ethylcyclopentane","5-Ethyl-1,1-dimethylcyclopentane"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The ring is the parent, and the substituents are numbered to get the lowest locant set.\nStarting at the methyl-bearing carbon gives {1,1,2}. Starting at the ethyl-bearing carbon gives {1,2,2}.\nAt the first point of difference, 1 is lower than 2, so {1,1,2} wins. The alphabetical rule applies only when the locant sets are tied.\nSubstituents are written alphabetically, with ethyl before methyl: 2-ethyl-1,1-dimethylcyclopentane.\nOption (3) breaks the alphabetical order. Option (4) numbers the ring the wrong way round.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":150,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Substituent names are arranged in alphabetical order in an IUPAC name. Which of the following statements about this ordering is correct?","image":"","options":["tert-Butyl is alphabetised under the letter 't'","Isopropyl is alphabetised under the letter 'i'","Neopentyl is alphabetised under the letter 'p'","sec-Butyl is alphabetised under the letter 's'"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The prefixes iso- and neo- are treated as part of the alkyl group's name, so they count when alphabetising.\nSo isopropyl is filed under 'i' (correct), and neopentyl is filed under 'n', not 'p'.\nThe prefixes sec- and tert- are ignored, so tert-butyl and sec-butyl are both filed under 'b'.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":151,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"For unbranched alkanes, the IUPAC name normally uses a numerical prefix for the number of carbon atoms. For which of the following alkanes is the prefix derived from a trivial name instead?","image":"","options":["Pentane","Hexane","Butane","Heptane"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that the names of straight-chain alkanes from $CH_4$ to $C_4H_{10}$ use prefixes taken from trivial names. These are meth-, eth-, prop- and but-.\nFrom $C_5H_{12}$ onward, the prefixes indicate the number of carbons directly: pent- (5), hex- (6), hept- (7).\nSo butane ($C_4H_{10}$) is the only option whose prefix is not a numerical one.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":152,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the inductive effect is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Groups such as $-NO_2$, $-CN$ and $-COOH$ show electron-withdrawing ($-I$) effect","Alkyl groups such as $-CH_3$ and $-C_2H_5$ are electron-donating ($+I$) groups","The inductive effect weakens rapidly with distance and is almost negligible beyond three bonds","The inductive effect involves displacement of π electrons along a conjugated chain"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: these groups pull electron density through σ bonds, which is a $-I$ effect.\n(2) Correct: alkyl groups are electron releasing, which is a $+I$ effect.\n(3) Correct: the polarisation fades quickly along the chain.\n(4) Incorrect: the inductive effect is a permanent shift of σ-bond electrons. Displacement of π electrons in a conjugated system is the resonance effect.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":153,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In the Carius method, 0.25 g of an organic compound gave 0.376 g of silver bromide. What is the percentage of bromine in the compound? (Atomic masses: Ag = 108, Br = 80)","image":"","options":["64.0%","32.0%","80.0%","42.6%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 0.25 g, m (AgBr) = 0.376 g, molar mass of AgBr = 188 g/mol.\nFormula: mass of Br = $\\frac{80 \\times m_{AgBr}}{188}$\nSubstituting: mass of Br = $\\frac{80 \\times 0.376}{188}$ = 0.16 g\n% Br = $\\frac{0.16}{0.25} \\times 100$\nSo % Br = 64.0%.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":154,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In steam distillation, steam is passed through a heated flask containing an organic liquid that is immiscible with water. At what point does the mixture boil?","image":"","options":["When the vapour pressure of the organic liquid alone becomes equal to the atmospheric pressure","When the sum of the vapour pressures of the organic liquid and water becomes equal to the atmospheric pressure","When the vapour pressure of water alone becomes equal to the atmospheric pressure","Only when the temperature of the mixture rises above 373 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In steam distillation, the organic liquid and water each contribute their own vapour pressure. Let these be $p_1$ and $p_2$.\nThe mixture boils when $p_1 + p_2$ equals the atmospheric pressure.\nBecause $p_1$ is less than atmospheric pressure, the mixture boils below the boiling point of the organic liquid. This lets heat-sensitive compounds such as aniline be distilled without decomposing.\nOption (3) fails because water alone reaches 1 atm only at 373 K. In fact, the mixture boils below 373 K, which rules out options (3) and (4).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":155,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In the direct estimation of oxygen, 0.30 g of an organic compound was decomposed by heating in a stream of N$_2$. The oxygen-containing products were passed over red-hot coke at 1373 K to give CO. This CO was then passed over warm I$_2$O$_5$, and the CO$_2$ formed weighed 0.33 g. What is the percentage of oxygen in the compound? (Atomic masses: C = 12, O = 16)","image":"","options":["40.0%","20.0%","80.0%","10.0%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Reactions: 2C + O$_2$ → 2CO, then I$_2$O$_5$ + 5CO → I$_2$ + 5CO$_2$.\nFrom these, 1 mol O$_2$ (32 g) gives 2 mol CO, which gives 2 mol CO$_2$ (88 g).\nFormula: mass of O = $\\frac{32 \\times m_{CO_2}}{88}$ = $\\frac{32 \\times 0.33}{88}$ = 0.12 g\n% O = $\\frac{0.12}{0.30} \\times 100$ = 40.0%\nUsing 32/44 instead of 32/88 gives the wrong value 80%.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":156,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the inductive effect is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["$-NO_2$ and $-CN$ groups are electron-withdrawing and show a $-I$ effect","Alkyl groups such as $-CH_3$ release electrons through σ bonds and show a $+I$ effect","The inductive effect becomes stronger as the number of intervening σ bonds between the group and the reference carbon increases","Halogens attached to a carbon chain show a $-I$ effect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The inductive effect is the polarisation of a σ bond caused by an adjacent polar bond. It is transmitted along the chain.\nIts magnitude falls off rapidly with distance. It is practically negligible after about three bonds, so option (3) is wrong.\nOptions (1) and (4) correctly list $-I$ groups, and option (2) correctly describes alkyl groups as $+I$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":157,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about thin layer chromatography (TLC) are correct?\n(i) A thin layer (about 0.2 mm) of silica gel or alumina is spread over a glass plate.\n(ii) The mixture is applied as a spot about 2 cm above one end of the plate.\n(iii) The component adsorbed more strongly on the adsorbent has the larger $R_f$ value.\n(iv) Spots of colourless compounds can be located by placing the plate in a covered jar containing iodine crystals.\n(v) Spots of amino acids can be detected by spraying the plate with ninhydrin solution.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is how NCERT describes the TLC plate (chromatoplate).\n(ii) Correct: the spot is placed about 2 cm above one end.\n(iii) Incorrect: a strongly adsorbed component moves less, so its $R_f$ is smaller.\n(iv) Correct: compounds that absorb iodine show up as brown spots.\n(v) Correct: ninhydrin is the spray reagent used to detect amino acids.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":158,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following species can act as nucleophiles?\n$HO^-$, $NC^-$, $H_2O$, $(CH_3)_3N$, $CH_3CH_2^+$, $(CH_3)_3C^-$, $BF_3$, $NO_2^+$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A nucleophile is an electron-pair donor. It may be negatively charged or neutral with a lone pair.\nNucleophiles: $HO^-$, $NC^-$ and the carbanion $(CH_3)_3C^-$ are negatively charged donors. $H_2O$ and $(CH_3)_3N$ are neutral but have lone pairs.\nElectrophiles: $CH_3CH_2^+$ (carbocation), $BF_3$ (electron-deficient boron) and $NO_2^+$ accept electron pairs.\nThe count of nucleophiles is 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":159,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Propan-1-ol ($CH_3CH_2CH_2OH$) and propan-2-ol ($CH_3CH(OH)CH_3$) are best described as:","image":"","options":["Chain isomers","Position isomers","Functional group isomers","Geometrical isomers"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Both compounds have the formula $C_3H_8O$ and the same carbon skeleton, a three-carbon chain.\nBoth contain the same functional group, –OH.\nThe only difference is where –OH is attached: on C-1 in one and on C-2 in the other.\nA difference in the position of a substituent or functional group on the same skeleton is position isomerism.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":160,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Aniline is purified by steam distillation at an atmospheric pressure of 760 mm Hg. At the temperature of distillation, the vapour pressure of water is 717 mm Hg. Assume the vapour composition is fixed by the partial pressures. What mass of aniline distils over per gram of water? (Molar masses: aniline = 93 g/mol, water = 18 g/mol)","image":"","options":["0.31 g","0.060 g","0.012 g","5.17 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $p_{total}$ = 760 mm Hg; $p_{water}$ = 717 mm Hg. So $p_{aniline}$ = 760 − 717 = 43 mm Hg.\nFormula: the mole ratio in the vapour equals the pressure ratio, so $\\frac{m_1}{m_2} = \\frac{p_1 M_1}{p_2 M_2}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{m_1}{m_2} = \\frac{43 \\times 93}{717 \\times 18} = \\frac{3999}{12906}$\nSo mass of aniline ≈ 0.31 g per gram of water. The value 0.060 is only the mole ratio, without converting to mass.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":161,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Which type of molecular model shows the relative size of each atom based on its van der Waals radius and does not show bonds?","image":"","options":["Framework model","Ball-and-stick model","Space-filling model","Bond-line model"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A space-filling model scales each atom to its van der Waals radius, so it conveys the volume each atom occupies. Bonds are not shown.\nA framework model shows only the bonds and not the atoms.\nA ball-and-stick model shows both the atoms (balls) and the bonds (sticks).\n'Bond-line' is a type of formula, not a molecular model.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":162,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about reactive intermediates formed by bond cleavage:\nI. Heterolysis of $(CH_3)_3C\\text{–}Br$ gives a carbocation whose vacant orbital is an unhybridised p orbital perpendicular to the plane of the three methyl groups.\nII. In a carbanion, the lone pair occupies an $sp^3$ hybrid orbital, and the species is pyramidal (distorted tetrahedral).\nIII. The carbocation formed by heterolysis of $CH_3CH_2\\text{–}Br$ is more stable than the one formed by heterolysis of $(CH_3)_2CH\\text{–}Br$.\nIV. Homolysis of a C–Br bond gives a carbon free radical and a bromide ion, $Br^-$.\nWhich of the statements are correct?","image":"","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I and IV only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"I. Correct: the tertiary carbocation is $sp^2$ hybridised, and its empty p orbital is perpendicular to the molecular plane.\nII. Correct: the carbanion carbon is $sp^3$ hybridised, and the lone pair makes the species pyramidal.\nIII. Incorrect: the ethyl cation (1°) is less stable than the isopropyl cation (2°), because stability follows 3° > 2° > 1°.\nIV. Incorrect: in homolysis each atom keeps one electron, so neutral $Br^\\bullet$ forms, not $Br^-$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":163,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"The sodium fusion extract of an organic compound is acidified with nitric acid, and silver nitrate is added. A yellow precipitate forms that is insoluble in ammonium hydroxide. The halogen present is:","image":"","options":["Fluorine","Chlorine","Bromine","Iodine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Halides in the extract form silver halides with $AgNO_3$.\nChlorine gives white AgCl, which is soluble in $NH_4OH$.\nBromine gives pale yellow AgBr, which is sparingly soluble in $NH_4OH$.\nIodine gives yellow AgI, which is insoluble in $NH_4OH$. This matches the observation.\nBromine is the tempting choice, but AgBr dissolves slightly in ammonia, while the given precipitate does not dissolve at all.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":164,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (alkyl group) with List-II (structure).\nList-I:\n(P) Isobutyl\n(Q) sec-Butyl\n(R) tert-Butyl\n(S) Neopentyl\nList-II:\n(1) $(CH_3)_3C$–\n(2) $(CH_3)_3C-CH_2$–\n(3) $(CH_3)_2CH-CH_2$–\n(4) $CH_3CH_2CH(CH_3)$–","image":"","options":["P-3, Q-4, R-1, S-2","P-4, Q-3, R-1, S-2","P-3, Q-4, R-2, S-1","P-1, Q-4, R-3, S-2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Isobutyl: the free valence is on a $CH_2$ attached to $CH(CH_3)_2$, which is structure (3).\nsec-Butyl: the free valence is on a secondary carbon, $CH_3CH_2CH(CH_3)$–, which is structure (4).\ntert-Butyl: the free valence is on a tertiary carbon, $(CH_3)_3C$–, which is structure (1).\nNeopentyl: $-CH_2C(CH_3)_3$, which is structure (2).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":165,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following groups shows a +I (electron-releasing) inductive effect rather than a –I effect?","image":"","options":["$-NO_2$","$-CN$","$-CH_2CH_3$","$-COOR$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The inductive effect arises from the electronegativity difference between a substituent and the carbon it is bonded to.\nThe –I (electron-withdrawing) groups listed in NCERT include halogens, $-NO_2$, $-CN$, $-COOH$, $-COOR$ and $-OC_6H_5$.\nAlkyl groups such as $-CH_3$ and $-CH_2CH_3$ are electron-donating, so they show a +I effect.\nThe nitro, cyano and ester groups all pull σ-electron density towards themselves, so they are –I groups.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":166,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Heterolytic cleavage of a C–X bond gives the tert-butyl cation, $(CH_3)_3C^+$. What are the hybridisation and shape of the positively charged carbon in this species?","image":"","options":["$sp^3$, pyramidal","$sp^2$, trigonal planar","$sp$, linear","$sp^3$, tetrahedral"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The cationic carbon uses three hybrid orbitals to form three σ bonds and carries no lone pair, so it is $sp^2$ hybridised.\nThe three bonds lie in one plane at 120°, so the shape is trigonal planar.\nThe vacant unhybridised p orbital lies perpendicular to this plane.\nThe tempting option '$sp^3$, pyramidal' describes a carbanion, where a lone pair occupies the fourth $sp^3$ orbital.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":167,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Which set lists, in this order, a benzenoid aromatic compound, a non-benzenoid aromatic compound and a heterocyclic aromatic compound?","image":"","options":["Tropolone, benzene, furan","Aniline, cyclohexene, thiophene","Naphthalene, tropolone, pyridine","Benzene, tetrahydrofuran, pyridine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Option (3) fits every position: naphthalene is benzenoid, tropolone is non-benzenoid aromatic and pyridine is heterocyclic aromatic.\nOption (1) fails because the first two compounds are in swapped positions.\nOption (2) fails because cyclohexene is alicyclic, not aromatic.\nOption (4) fails because tetrahydrofuran is an alicyclic heterocycle, not a non-benzenoid aromatic.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":168,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (separation task) with List-II (most suitable technique).\nList-I:\n(a) Recovering glycerol from spent-lye in the soap industry\n(b) Separating aniline from an aniline–water mixture\n(c) Separating crude oil into its fractions\n(d) Purifying camphor containing a non-volatile impurity\nList-II:\n(i) Sublimation\n(ii) Steam distillation\n(iii) Distillation under reduced pressure\n(iv) Fractional distillation","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Glycerol decomposes at its normal boiling point, so it is distilled at lower temperature under reduced pressure: (iii).\n(b) Aniline is steam volatile and immiscible with water, so steam distillation is used: (ii).\n(c) Crude oil fractions have close boiling points, so they are separated by fractional distillation in a column: (iv).\n(d) Camphor sublimes and leaves the non-volatile impurity behind: (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-169","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":169,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the reaction $CH_3CH_2Br + HO^- \\rightarrow CH_3CH_2OH + Br^-$.\nWhich of the following statements about it is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["$CH_3CH_2Br$ is the substrate and $HO^-$ is the reagent","The carbon bonded to Br is electrophilic because Br is more electronegative than carbon","The C–Br bond breaks heterolytically, with bromine keeping the shared electron pair","The C–Br bond breaks homolytically, so the reaction is a free radical reaction"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the substrate's carbon forms the new bond, and the attacking species HO⁻ is the reagent.\n(2) Correct: polarisation of $C^{\\delta+}–Br^{\\delta-}$ makes this carbon electron deficient.\n(3) Correct: Br leaves as Br⁻, so it carries away the bonding pair. This is heterolytic cleavage.\n(4) Incorrect: ions are formed, so this is an ionic (polar) reaction, not a free radical one.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-170","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":170,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"When $H^+$ attacks an alkene, the π-electrons of the double bond shift towards the carbon atom to which the $H^+$ becomes attached. This displacement is an example of:","image":"","options":["+E effect","–E effect","+R effect","Hyperconjugation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The electromeric effect is a temporary, complete transfer of the shared π-electrons of a multiple bond. It occurs only when an attacking reagent is present.\nIf the π-electrons move towards the atom to which the reagent attaches, the effect is called +E.\nAddition of an acid ($H^+$) to an alkene is NCERT's example of the +E effect.\nIn the –E effect, the electrons move away from the atom to which the reagent attaches, as when a nucleophile attacks a carbonyl carbon.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-171","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":171,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (compound) with List-II (class):\nList-I:\n(A) Tetrahydrofuran\n(B) Cyclohexane\n(C) Naphthalene\n(D) Thiophene\nList-II:\n(I) Benzenoid aromatic\n(II) Heterocyclic aromatic\n(III) Alicyclic, homocyclic\n(IV) Alicyclic, heterocyclic","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Tetrahydrofuran is a saturated ring containing O, so it is alicyclic and heterocyclic (IV).\n(B) Cyclohexane has a ring made only of carbon atoms, so it is alicyclic and homocyclic (III).\n(C) Naphthalene contains fused benzene rings, so it is benzenoid aromatic (I).\n(D) Thiophene has an aromatic ring containing S, so it is heterocyclic aromatic (II).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-172","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":172,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The electromeric effect is temporary and operates only when an attacking reagent is present.\n(ii) The inductive effect is a permanent effect transmitted along a chain of σ bonds.\n(iii) Hyperconjugation involves delocalisation of σ electrons of a C–H bond into an adjacent empty p orbital or π orbital.\n(iv) Individual resonance (canonical) structures are hypothetical and do not represent any real molecule.\n(v) The $-NO_2$ group shows a +R effect when attached to a conjugated system.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the electromeric effect is a temporary shift of π electrons, operating only in the presence of a reagent.\n(ii) Correct: the inductive effect is permanent and operates through σ bonds, weakening with distance.\n(iii) Correct: this is the definition of hyperconjugation, and it explains carbocation stability.\n(iv) Correct: canonical forms are hypothetical, and only the hybrid is real.\n(v) Incorrect: $-NO_2$ withdraws π electrons, so it shows a −R effect.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-173","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":173,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The IUPAC name of NC–CH$_2$–CH$_2$–COOH is 3-cyanopropanoic acid.\nStatement II: In the IUPAC priority order, –COOH ranks higher than –CN.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement II: correct. –COOH sits at the top of the priority order, above –CN.\nStatement I: correct.\n• –COOH is the suffix, and its carbon is C-1.\n• The two CH$_2$ groups are C-2 and C-3, which gives a propanoic acid chain.\n• The carbon of the –CN group is not counted in the chain because –CN is named as the prefix cyano.\n• –CN is attached at C-3, so the name is 3-cyanopropanoic acid.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-174","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":174,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of structural isomerism) with List-II (pair of isomers).\nList-I:\n(a) Chain isomerism\n(b) Position isomerism\n(c) Functional group isomerism\n(d) Metamerism\nList-II:\n(i) Propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol\n(ii) Pentane and 2-methylbutane\n(iii) Diethyl ether and methyl propyl ether\n(iv) Propanal and propanone","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a)-(ii): Pentane and 2-methylbutane ($C_5H_{12}$) differ in the carbon skeleton.\n(b)-(i): In the two propanols, only the position of –OH changes.\n(c)-(iv): Propanal (aldehyde) and propanone (ketone), both $C_3H_6O$, have different functional groups.\n(d)-(iii): The two ethers have different alkyl groups on either side of the O atom, which is metamerism.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-175","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":175,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following carbocations in increasing order of stability:\n(I) $CH_3^+$\n(II) $(CH_3)_3C^+$\n(III) $CH_3CH_2^+$\n(IV) $(CH_3)_2CH^+$","image":"","options":["I < III < IV < II","II < IV < III < I","I < IV < III < II","III < I < IV < II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Alkyl groups stabilise the positive centre through the +I effect and hyperconjugation.\nSo stability increases as more alkyl groups are attached to the cationic carbon.\nMethyl (I, no alkyl groups) < ethyl, 1° (III) < isopropyl, 2° (IV) < tert-butyl, 3° (II).\nThe order is I < III < IV < II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-176","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":176,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"On the basis of hyperconjugation, the correct order of stability of the following carbocations is:","image":"","options":["$(CH_3)_3C^+ > (CH_3)_2CH^+ > CH_3CH_2^+ > CH_3^+$","$CH_3^+ > CH_3CH_2^+ > (CH_3)_2CH^+ > (CH_3)_3C^+$","$(CH_3)_2CH^+ > (CH_3)_3C^+ > CH_3CH_2^+ > CH_3^+$","$CH_3CH_2^+ > (CH_3)_2CH^+ > CH_3^+ > (CH_3)_3C^+$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In hyperconjugation, each α-C–H bond can donate σ-electron density into the empty p orbital of the cationic carbon.\nCount the α-hydrogens in each ion:\n• $(CH_3)_3C^+$ has 9.\n• $(CH_3)_2CH^+$ has 6.\n• $CH_3CH_2^+$ has 3.\n• $CH_3^+$ has 0.\nMore α-H atoms give more hyperconjugative structures, greater dispersal of the positive charge and greater stability.\nThe tertiary cation is therefore the most stable and the methyl cation the least stable.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-177","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":177,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The actual energy of benzene is lower than the energy of any of its canonical (Kekulé) structures, and this difference is called the resonance energy.\nStatement II: Canonical structures are hypothetical, and none of them individually represents the real molecule.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The resonance hybrid is more stable than any single contributing structure. The energy difference between the actual structure and the lowest-energy canonical structure is the resonance stabilisation energy.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT states that canonical structures are hypothetical. The real molecule is the hybrid of these structures.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-178","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":178,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (pair of compounds) with List-II (type of isomerism).\nList-I:\n(A) Pentane and 2-methylbutane\n(B) Propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol\n(C) Propanal and propanone\n(D) Ethoxyethane and 1-methoxypropane\nList-II:\n(i) Metamerism\n(ii) Chain isomerism\n(iii) Functional group isomerism\n(iv) Position isomerism","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(A) Pentane and 2-methylbutane differ in the carbon skeleton, so they are chain isomers: (ii).\n(B) Both are alcohols with –OH on different carbons, so they are position isomers: (iv).\n(C) Propanal is an aldehyde and propanone is a ketone, both $C_3H_6O$. They are functional group isomers: (iii).\n(D) Both are ethers, but the alkyl groups on oxygen differ (ethyl/ethyl vs methyl/propyl). This is metamerism: (i).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-179","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":179,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"What is the correct IUPAC name of the alkane $CH_3CH_2CH(C_2H_5)CH_2CH[CH(CH_3)CH(CH_3)_2]CH_2CH_2CH_2CH_2CH_3$?","image":"","options":["3-Ethyl-5-(1,2-dimethylpropyl)decane","5-(1,2-Dimethylpropyl)-3-ethyldecane","6-(1,2-Dimethylpropyl)-8-ethyldecane","5-(2,3-Dimethylpropyl)-3-ethyldecane"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The longest chain has 10 carbons, so the parent is decane. Numbering from the left gives substituents at C-3 and C-5, which beats {6,8} from the right.\nThe branched substituent is numbered starting from the carbon attached to the main chain: $-CH(CH_3)CH(CH_3)CH_3$. This gives methyl groups at positions 1 and 2, so it is 1,2-dimethylpropyl. The name '2,3-dimethylpropyl' comes from numbering the wrong way.\nA complex substituent is alphabetised by the first letter of its complete name, here 'd' for dimethylpropyl. Since d comes before e, it is written before ethyl.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-180","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":180,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The bond-line formula of cyclohexane is drawn simply as a regular hexagon, without writing any C or H symbols.\nReason (R): In a bond-line formula, each junction of lines represents a carbon atom bearing enough hydrogen atoms to satisfy its tetravalence.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Cyclic compounds are shown in bond-line form as polygons, and cyclohexane is a hexagon.\nR is true. Each vertex is a carbon, and hydrogens are understood to complete its four bonds.\nEach vertex of the hexagon is therefore a $CH_2$, which is exactly why no symbols need to be written. So R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-181","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":181,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"In Kjeldahl's method, the ammonia evolved from 0.60 g of an organic compound was absorbed in 50 mL of 0.5 M H$_2$SO$_4$. The residual acid required 60 mL of 0.5 M NaOH for complete neutralisation. What is the percentage of nitrogen in the compound? (Atomic mass: N = 14)","image":"","options":["46.7%","23.3%","70.0%","35.0%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: H$_2$SO$_4$ taken = 0.050 × 0.5 = 0.025 mol. NaOH used = 0.060 × 0.5 = 0.030 mol.\nNaOH reacts with H$_2$SO$_4$ in a 2 : 1 ratio, so the residual acid = 0.015 mol.\nAcid used by NH$_3$ = 0.025 − 0.015 = 0.010 mol. This reacts with 2 × 0.010 = 0.020 mol NH$_3$.\nMass of N = 0.020 × 14 = 0.28 g\n% N = $\\frac{0.28}{0.60} \\times 100$ = 46.7%\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-182","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":182,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"What is the correct IUPAC name of $OHC-CH_2-CH_2-COOH$?","image":"","options":["3-Formylpropanoic acid","4-Oxobutanoic acid","4-Carboxybutanal","1-Oxobutanoic acid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: Find the principal group. $-COOH$ has higher priority than $-CHO$, so the suffix is -oic acid.\nStep 2: Find the chain. Both carbonyl carbons belong to the main chain, giving 4 carbons, so the parent is butanoic acid. The COOH carbon is C-1.\nStep 3: Name the other group. The $-CHO$ carbon is C-4. Its =O is cited as the prefix 'oxo', giving 4-oxo.\n'3-Formylpropanoic acid' is wrong because it leaves a chain carbon out of the parent chain.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ORGANIC-BASICS-183","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","qno":183,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The methyl carbanion, $CH_3^-$, has a pyramidal shape.\nReason (R): The carbanionic carbon is $sp^3$ hybridised, and one of its $sp^3$ orbitals is occupied by an unshared electron pair.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the carbanion $CH_3^-$ is pyramidal, much like ammonia.\nR is true: the carbon uses four $sp^3$ orbitals. Three of them form C–H bonds and the fourth holds the lone pair.\nA lone pair occupying one tetrahedral position is exactly what produces a pyramidal shape, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 8 · Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques","difficulty":"medium"}]});
